{"parentUuid":"6cb69157-6e40-4bd8-859d-8adafecfd3ca","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0a8de032-5fd3-4106-bcf4-797ed6a2efc2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_l3YgaoJ2USFLFRLiUJ4N0kTy","type":"tool_result","content":"140\t * found where it is rendered, but it is capped: without a cap a message made of\n141\t * many one-character lines would widen the search to \"anywhere in the viewport\",\n142\t * which is the property the banding exists to deny.\n143\t */\n144\texport const MESSAGE_ECHO_MAX_WINDOW_LINES = 16;\n145\t\n146\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n147\t// guard classification — enforced, not documentation\n148\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n149\t\n150\texport type GuardClass =\n151\t\t/** Outside the unauthenticated hook's reach. Only these may permit a write. */\n152\t\t| \"load_bearing\"\n153\t\t/** Fed by `notifications.hook`. May refuse; may never permit. */\n154\t\t| \"forgeable\"\n155\t\t/** Absence is a sound veto; presence is never evidence. */\n156\t\t| \"veto_only\"\n157\t\t/** Neither forgeable nor sufficient — a sanity condition. */\n158\t\t| \"supporting\";\n159\t\n160\texport const GUARD_CLASSES: Readonly<Record<AnswerGuardName, GuardClass>> = {\n161\t\ttranscript: \"load_bearing\",\n162\t\tscreen: \"load_bearing\",\n163\t\tbinding: \"forgeable\",\n164\t\tpermission_axis: \"forgeable\",\n165\t\tsession: \"supporting\",\n166\t\taskq_marker: \"veto_only\",\n167\t};\n168\t\n169\t/**\n170\t * (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) Forgeable guards that ABSTAIN rather than refuse once every\n171\t * load-bearing guard has passed.\n172\t *\n173\t * \"May refuse; may never permit\" is the right rule for a forgeable source in\n174\t * general, and it stays the rule for `binding` — which proves the captured\n175\t * question belongs to the agent session currently on the terminal, something\n176\t * neither the transcript nor the screen says anything about.\n177\t *\n178\t * `permission_axis` is different in two ways that together make its refusal\n179\t * worth less than the honest answers it was costing:\n180\t *\n181\t *   - IT ADDS NOTHING. The axis is `lastEventType === PermissionRequest` — \"a\n182\t *     permission request is the most recent thing this terminal reported\". By\n183\t *     the time it is read, guard 1 has proved the tool call is still unanswered\n184\t *     in the agent's own transcript and guard 5 has proved THIS question's\n185\t *     picker is on screen right now with the pressed row where the capture says\n186\t *     it is. Those two prove a pending permission request more directly than a\n187\t *     single latched enum ever did.\n188\t *   - IT IS A LATCH, AND ITS REFUSAL IS ITSELF FORGEABLE. Any later hook event\n189\t *     overwrites `lastEventType`, so an ordinary race between capture and answer\n190\t *     clears it and the wire code for that is indistinguishable from staleness.\n191\t *     And the same unauthenticated localhost hook that could forge the axis to\n192\t *     `true` can clear it to `false` — so leaving it able to refuse hands an\n193\t *     attacker a denial primitive while giving the defence nothing, which is the\n194\t *     wrong side of every trade in this file.\n195\t *\n196\t * The abstain is CONDITIONAL and the condition is checked, not assumed: it\n197\t * applies only when every guard in `LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS` has actually passed.\n198\t * The evaluation order already guarantees that at the point guard 4 runs, but a\n199\t * reordering must break the build's behaviour loudly rather than silently widen\n200\t * this, so the condition is evaluated from `evaluation` rather than inferred\n201\t * from position.\n202\t */\n203\texport const ABSTAINING_GUARDS: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = [\n204\t\t\"permission_axis\",\n205\t];\n206\t\n207\texport const LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = (\n208\t\tObject.keys(GUARD_CLASSES) as AnswerGuardName[]\n209\t).filter((name) => GUARD_CLASSES[name] === \"load_bearing\");\n210\t\n211\t/**\n212\t * The order `evaluateGuards` MUST run the stack in, as data rather than as a\n213\t * hand-written sequence of statements.\n214\t *\n215\t * The classification above is only worth anything if the ordering it implies is\n216\t * mechanical. It was not: `assertGuardClassification` used to check nothing but\n217\t * \"at least one load-bearing guard exists\", while the header claimed a forgeable\n218\t * guard \"can never contribute to a permission\" and named that function as what\n219\t * kept it honest. The ordering happened to be correct; nothing made it stay\n220\t * correct, and a future guard classified `forgeable` and inserted early — the\n221\t * natural thing to do if it is cheap — would have been consulted on a path that\n222\t * permits, which is precisely what the classification exists to deny.\n223\t *\n224\t * Now: this array is asserted at module load to place every `load_bearing` guard\n225\t * ahead of every other class, and `evaluateGuards` asserts that it walks the\n226\t * stack in exactly this order, so drift is a crash rather than a silent\n227\t * weakening.\n228\t */\n229\texport const GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = [\n230\t\t\"transcript\",\n231\t\t\"screen\",\n232\t\t\"session\",\n233\t\t\"binding\",\n234\t\t\"permission_axis\",\n235\t\t\"askq_marker\",\n236\t];\n237\t\n238\t/**\n239\t * Run at module load. A guard added to `AnswerGuardName` without a class here is\n240\t * a compile error; a stack with no load-bearing guard at all, one that\n241\t * `GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER` does not cover exactly, or an order that consults a\n242\t * forgeable guard before a load-bearing one is a startup crash. All of them beat\n243\t * discovering it after an answer landed on the wrong screen.\n244\t */\n245\tfunction assertGuardClassification(): void {\n246\t\tif (LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS.length === 0) {\n247\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n248\t\t\t\t\"(COMPANION-BRIDGE) the answer guard stack has no load-bearing guard; refusing to load\",\n249\t\t\t);\n250\t\t}\n251\t\tconst classified = Object.keys(GUARD_CLASSES) as AnswerGuardName[];\n252\t\tconst ordered = new Set(GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER);\n253\t\tif (ordered.size !== GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.length) {\n254\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n255\t\t\t\t\"(COMPANION-BRIDGE) GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER lists a guard twice; refusing to load\",\n256\t\t\t);\n257\t\t}\n258\t\tfor (const name of classified) {\n259\t\t\tif (!ordered.has(name)) {\n260\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n261\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} is classified but never evaluated; refusing to load`,\n262\t\t\t\t);\n263\t\t\t}\n264\t\t}\n265\t\tfor (const name of GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER) {\n266\t\t\tif (!classified.includes(name)) {\n267\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n268\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} is evaluated but not classified; refusing to load`,\n269\t\t\t\t);\n270\t\t\t}\n271\t\t}\n272\t\tlet seenNonLoadBearing: AnswerGuardName | null = null;\n273\t\tfor (const name of GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER) {\n274\t\t\tif (GUARD_CLASSES[name] === \"load_bearing\") {\n275\t\t\t\tif (seenNonLoadBearing !== null) {\n276\t\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n277\t\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) load-bearing guard ${name} is evaluated after ${seenNonLoadBearing} (${GUARD_CLASSES[seenNonLoadBearing]}); a guard that may never permit must not be consulted before one that does. Refusing to load`,\n278\t\t\t\t\t);\n279\t\t\t\t}\n280\t\t\t\tcontinue;\n281\t\t\t}\n282\t\t\tseenNonLoadBearing = name;\n283\t\t}\n284\t\t// (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) The abstain list was inert: it was a name the branch below\n285\t\t// happened to check against, with nothing stopping a guard being added to it\n286\t\t// that is load-bearing, or that runs BEFORE the guards whose passing is the\n287\t\t// entire premise of abstaining. Both are now startup crashes, so the list\n288\t\t// carries the weight the abstain branch gives it.\n289\t\tconst lastLoadBearing = Math.max(\n290\t\t\t...LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS.map((name) => GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.indexOf(name)),\n291\t\t);\n292\t\tfor (const name of ABSTAINING_GUARDS) {\n293\t\t\tif (GUARD_CLASSES[name] !== \"forgeable\") {\n294\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n295\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is classified ${GUARD_CLASSES[name]}; only a forgeable guard — one whose refusal is as forgeable as its permission would be — may abstain. Refusing to load`,\n296\t\t\t\t);\n297\t\t\t}\n298\t\t\tconst position = GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.indexOf(name);\n299\t\t\tif (position < 0) {\n300\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n301\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is never evaluated; refusing to load`,\n302\t\t\t\t);\n303\t\t\t}\n304\t\t\tif (position < lastLoadBearing) {\n305\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n306\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is evaluated at position ${position}, before the load-bearing guard at ${lastLoadBearing}; abstaining is only sound once every load-bearing guard has PASSED. Refusing to load`,\n307\t\t\t\t);\n308\t\t\t}\n309\t\t}\n310\t}\n311\tassertGuardClassification();\n312\t\n313\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n314\t// dependencies\n315\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n316\t\n317\t/** What a guard source reports. `null` means \"could not determine\" and fails closed. */\n318\texport type GuardSourceResult = boolean | null;\n319\t\n320\texport interface TerminalAgentInfo {\n321\t\tkind: AgentKind;\n322\t\t/** false => a plain shell. NEVER writable. */\n323\t\tbound: boolean;\n324\t\t/** The agent session this terminal is currently bound to, if known. */\n325\t\tagentSessionId: string | null;\n326\t}\n327\t\n328\t/**\n329\t * The host.db ids behind an opaque wire handle. These — and ONLY these — are\n330\t * what the pty writer accepts; the wire handle is a truncated SHA-256 and is\n331\t * never a key in the live session map.\n332\t */\n333\texport interface HostTerminalRef {\n334\t\t/** host.db `terminal_sessions.id`. */\n335\t\thostTerminalId: string;\n336\t\t/** host.db `workspaces.id`. */\n337\t\thostWorkspaceId: string;\n338\t}\n339\t\n340\t/**\n341\t * Everything the answer path needs from the rest of host-service.\n342\t *\n343\t * Note the two writers are deliberately DIFFERENT SHAPES so neither can be\n344\t * passed where the other belongs:\n345\t *  - `writer` is a `RawPtyWriter`, mintable only by `createRawPtyWriter`, which\n346\t *    requires the acknowledged raw-writer runtime marker. Bracketed paste is\n347\t *    INERT against the picker, while fire-and-forget input cannot report daemon\n348\t *    refusal, so either wrong writer fails at bridge startup;\n349\t *  - `writeFramed` is async and takes `{text, submit}`, and is used ONLY by"}]},"uuid":"6c8fa9b0-dc30-43cc-9f2b-a306fae07e50","timestamp":"2026-08-10T22:37:55.261Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\answer.ts","content":" * found where it is rendered, but it is capped: without a cap a message made of\n * many one-character lines would widen the search to \"anywhere in the viewport\",\n * which is the property the banding exists to deny.\n */\nexport const MESSAGE_ECHO_MAX_WINDOW_LINES = 16;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// guard classification — enforced, not documentation\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nexport type GuardClass =\n\t/** Outside the unauthenticated hook's reach. Only these may permit a write. */\n\t| \"load_bearing\"\n\t/** Fed by `notifications.hook`. May refuse; may never permit. */\n\t| \"forgeable\"\n\t/** Absence is a sound veto; presence is never evidence. */\n\t| \"veto_only\"\n\t/** Neither forgeable nor sufficient — a sanity condition. */\n\t| \"supporting\";\n\nexport const GUARD_CLASSES: Readonly<Record<AnswerGuardName, GuardClass>> = {\n\ttranscript: \"load_bearing\",\n\tscreen: \"load_bearing\",\n\tbinding: \"forgeable\",\n\tpermission_axis: \"forgeable\",\n\tsession: \"supporting\",\n\taskq_marker: \"veto_only\",\n};\n\n/**\n * (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) Forgeable guards that ABSTAIN rather than refuse once every\n * load-bearing guard has passed.\n *\n * \"May refuse; may never permit\" is the right rule for a forgeable source in\n * general, and it stays the rule for `binding` — which proves the captured\n * question belongs to the agent session currently on the terminal, something\n * neither the transcript nor the screen says anything about.\n *\n * `permission_axis` is different in two ways that together make its refusal\n * worth less than the honest answers it was costing:\n *\n *   - IT ADDS NOTHING. The axis is `lastEventType === PermissionRequest` — \"a\n *     permission request is the most recent thing this terminal reported\". By\n *     the time it is read, guard 1 has proved the tool call is still unanswered\n *     in the agent's own transcript and guard 5 has proved THIS question's\n *     picker is on screen right now with the pressed row where the capture says\n *     it is. Those two prove a pending permission request more directly than a\n *     single latched enum ever did.\n *   - IT IS A LATCH, AND ITS REFUSAL IS ITSELF FORGEABLE. Any later hook event\n *     overwrites `lastEventType`, so an ordinary race between capture and answer\n *     clears it and the wire code for that is indistinguishable from staleness.\n *     And the same unauthenticated localhost hook that could forge the axis to\n *     `true` can clear it to `false` — so leaving it able to refuse hands an\n *     attacker a denial primitive while giving the defence nothing, which is the\n *     wrong side of every trade in this file.\n *\n * The abstain is CONDITIONAL and the condition is checked, not assumed: it\n * applies only when every guard in `LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS` has actually passed.\n * The evaluation order already guarantees that at the point guard 4 runs, but a\n * reordering must break the build's behaviour loudly rather than silently widen\n * this, so the condition is evaluated from `evaluation` rather than inferred\n * from position.\n */\nexport const ABSTAINING_GUARDS: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = [\n\t\"permission_axis\",\n];\n\nexport const LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = (\n\tObject.keys(GUARD_CLASSES) as AnswerGuardName[]\n).filter((name) => GUARD_CLASSES[name] === \"load_bearing\");\n\n/**\n * The order `evaluateGuards` MUST run the stack in, as data rather than as a\n * hand-written sequence of statements.\n *\n * The classification above is only worth anything if the ordering it implies is\n * mechanical. It was not: `assertGuardClassification` used to check nothing but\n * \"at least one load-bearing guard exists\", while the header claimed a forgeable\n * guard \"can never contribute to a permission\" and named that function as what\n * kept it honest. The ordering happened to be correct; nothing made it stay\n * correct, and a future guard classified `forgeable` and inserted early — the\n * natural thing to do if it is cheap — would have been consulted on a path that\n * permits, which is precisely what the classification exists to deny.\n *\n * Now: this array is asserted at module load to place every `load_bearing` guard\n * ahead of every other class, and `evaluateGuards` asserts that it walks the\n * stack in exactly this order, so drift is a crash rather than a silent\n * weakening.\n */\nexport const GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER: readonly AnswerGuardName[] = [\n\t\"transcript\",\n\t\"screen\",\n\t\"session\",\n\t\"binding\",\n\t\"permission_axis\",\n\t\"askq_marker\",\n];\n\n/**\n * Run at module load. A guard added to `AnswerGuardName` without a class here is\n * a compile error; a stack with no load-bearing guard at all, one that\n * `GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER` does not cover exactly, or an order that consults a\n * forgeable guard before a load-bearing one is a startup crash. All of them beat\n * discovering it after an answer landed on the wrong screen.\n */\nfunction assertGuardClassification(): void {\n\tif (LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS.length === 0) {\n\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\"(COMPANION-BRIDGE) the answer guard stack has no load-bearing guard; refusing to load\",\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tconst classified = Object.keys(GUARD_CLASSES) as AnswerGuardName[];\n\tconst ordered = new Set(GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER);\n\tif (ordered.size !== GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.length) {\n\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\"(COMPANION-BRIDGE) GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER lists a guard twice; refusing to load\",\n\t\t);\n\t}\n\tfor (const name of classified) {\n\t\tif (!ordered.has(name)) {\n\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} is classified but never evaluated; refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor (const name of GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER) {\n\t\tif (!classified.includes(name)) {\n\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} is evaluated but not classified; refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tlet seenNonLoadBearing: AnswerGuardName | null = null;\n\tfor (const name of GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER) {\n\t\tif (GUARD_CLASSES[name] === \"load_bearing\") {\n\t\t\tif (seenNonLoadBearing !== null) {\n\t\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) load-bearing guard ${name} is evaluated after ${seenNonLoadBearing} (${GUARD_CLASSES[seenNonLoadBearing]}); a guard that may never permit must not be consulted before one that does. Refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t\t);\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tcontinue;\n\t\t}\n\t\tseenNonLoadBearing = name;\n\t}\n\t// (GUARD4-ABSTAIN) The abstain list was inert: it was a name the branch below\n\t// happened to check against, with nothing stopping a guard being added to it\n\t// that is load-bearing, or that runs BEFORE the guards whose passing is the\n\t// entire premise of abstaining. Both are now startup crashes, so the list\n\t// carries the weight the abstain branch gives it.\n\tconst lastLoadBearing = Math.max(\n\t\t...LOAD_BEARING_GUARDS.map((name) => GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.indexOf(name)),\n\t);\n\tfor (const name of ABSTAINING_GUARDS) {\n\t\tif (GUARD_CLASSES[name] !== \"forgeable\") {\n\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is classified ${GUARD_CLASSES[name]}; only a forgeable guard — one whose refusal is as forgeable as its permission would be — may abstain. Refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tconst position = GUARD_EVALUATION_ORDER.indexOf(name);\n\t\tif (position < 0) {\n\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is never evaluated; refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (position < lastLoadBearing) {\n\t\t\tthrow new Error(\n\t\t\t\t`(COMPANION-BRIDGE) guard ${name} may abstain but is evaluated at position ${position}, before the load-bearing guard at ${lastLoadBearing}; abstaining is only sound once every load-bearing guard has PASSED. Refusing to load`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\nassertGuardClassification();\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// dependencies\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n/** What a guard source reports. `null` means \"could not determine\" and fails closed. */\nexport type GuardSourceResult = boolean | null;\n\nexport interface TerminalAgentInfo {\n\tkind: AgentKind;\n\t/** false => a plain shell. NEVER writable. */\n\tbound: boolean;\n\t/** The agent session this terminal is currently bound to, if known. */\n\tagentSessionId: string | null;\n}\n\n/**\n * The host.db ids behind an opaque wire handle. These — and ONLY these — are\n * what the pty writer accepts; the wire handle is a truncated SHA-256 and is\n * never a key in the live session map.\n */\nexport interface HostTerminalRef {\n\t/** host.db `terminal_sessions.id`. */\n\thostTerminalId: string;\n\t/** host.db `workspaces.id`. */\n\thostWorkspaceId: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * Everything the answer path needs from the rest of host-service.\n *\n * Note the two writers are deliberately DIFFERENT SHAPES so neither can be\n * passed where the other belongs:\n *  - `writer` is a `RawPtyWriter`, mintable only by `createRawPtyWriter`, which\n *    requires the acknowledged raw-writer runtime marker. Bracketed paste is\n *    INERT against the picker, while fire-and-forget input cannot report daemon\n *    refusal, so either wrong writer fails at bridge startup;\n *  - `writeFramed` is async and takes `{text, submit}`, and is used ONLY by","numLines":210,"startLine":140,"totalLines":4259}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6cb69157-6e40-4bd8-859d-8adafecfd3ca","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.226","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
