{"parentUuid":"9f3669f5-2d8e-4c29-bd92-b7f8c9b03882","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_eJ3I8I2z9r31w3r4wod7I9xH","type":"tool_result","content":"40\t * transaction, which a read-modify-rewrite of a JSON file structurally is not —\n41\t * two processes, or one process and its own overtaken request, cannot be\n42\t * serialised by rewriting a file.\n43\t *\n44\t * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n45\t * WHY SQLITE, AND WHAT IT BUYS BEYOND THE TRANSACTION\n46\t * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n47\t * The file store's durability rested on an INFERENCE about NTFS: that a content\n48\t * `fsync` publishes an earlier rename in the same directory, because both sit in\n49\t * the volume metadata log. Microsoft documents `FlushFileBuffers` as flushing the\n50\t * SPECIFIED FILE, and the documented durable-rename primitive\n51\t * (`MoveFileEx(MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH)`) is one libuv never passes. The whole\n52\t * witness mechanism existed to detect the consequence of that inference being\n53\t * wrong, and could not detect the case where both files rolled back together.\n54\t *\n55\t * SQLite's durability is documented rather than inferred — see the `synchronous`\n56\t * assertion below, which is load-bearing and checked rather than assumed.\n57\t *\n58\t * A second, quieter win: the JSON file was validated as a WHOLE, so ONE\n59\t * incoherent record failed the file schema and quarantined every other record's\n60\t * 24 hours with it. Rows are validated individually here, so a corrupt row costs\n61\t * exactly one `requestId` its status.\n62\t *\n63\t * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n64\t * WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO\n65\t * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n66\t * It does not decide what the CLIENT renders. It returns discriminated outcomes\n67\t * and the honest-state discipline stays where it was: the five statuses mean\n68\t * exactly what §11.5 says, nothing collapses into a vague \"sent\", and a state\n69\t * this module cannot prove is never reported as terminal.\n70\t */\n71\t\n72\timport { and, eq, sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n73\timport type { AnswerAttemptStatus, HostDb } from \"../db\";\n74\timport { answerAttempts, answerCoverageEpoch } from \"../db\";\n75\timport { LOG_PREFIX } from \"./config\";\n76\timport {\n77\t\tassertDurableSqlite,\n78\t\tbase64UrlEncode,\n79\t\tisCanonicalWireId,\n80\t\trandomBytes,\n81\t} from \"./crypto\";\n82\timport type {\n83\t\tAnswerGuardName,\n84\t\tEpochMs,\n85\t\tRequestId,\n86\t\tSealedErrorCode,\n87\t} from \"./types\";\n88\timport { ANSWER_GUARD_NAMES } from \"./types\";\n89\t\n90\t/** Bytes of randomness in a coverage epoch. 16 matches the install generation. */\n91\tconst COVERAGE_EPOCH_BYTES = 16;\n92\t\n93\t/** The single-row epoch table's only key. */\n94\tconst EPOCH_ROW_ID = 1;\n95\t\n96\t/**\n97\t * Every failure code the ledger may PERSIST, and the set the read boundary\n98\t * enforces.\n99\t *\n100\t * Wider than the old JSON store's two codes (`guard_failed`, `internal`) on\n101\t * purpose. Since the claim moved to the top of `handleAnswer`, a request can be\n102\t * refused for a reason the store never used to see — the panic write-disable, a\n103\t * stale question, a lost lease, an unusable agent binding — and §11.4 says a replay\n104\t * returns the RECORDED outcome. Recording `internal` for a `write_disabled`\n105\t * refusal would make that replay lie about why, so the real code is stored and\n106\t * rethrown verbatim.\n107\t *\n108\t * Every member is a §10 sealed code, so a stored value is always something a client\n109\t * already knows how to render.\n110\t */\n111\tconst LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES = [\n112\t\t\"stale_question\",\n113\t\t\"already_resolved\",\n114\t\t\"request_closed\",\n115\t\t\"lease_held\",\n116\t\t\"guard_failed\",\n117\t\t\"picker_open\",\n118\t\t\"capability_unsupported\",\n119\t\t\"write_disabled\",\n120\t\t\"bad_request\",\n121\t\t\"internal\",\n122\t] as const satisfies readonly SealedErrorCode[];\n123\t\n124\t/** A code the ledger can store, narrowed from §10's sealed set. */\n125\texport type LedgerFailureCode = (typeof LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES)[number];\n126\t\n127\t/**\n128\t * The query surface this module uses, which BOTH the database and a transaction\n129\t * satisfy.\n130\t *\n131\t * Deliberately structural rather than `tx as HostDb`: a transaction genuinely is\n132\t * not a `HostDb` — it has no `$client` — and casting one to the other would have\n133\t * been a lie that compiled. Every helper below takes this, so the same code runs\n134\t * inside and outside a transaction and cannot accidentally reach for a\n135\t * connection-level method (like the `synchronous` pragma) from inside one.\n136\t */\n137\ttype Queryable = Pick<HostDb, \"select\" | \"insert\" | \"update\" | \"delete\">;\n138\t\n139\t/**\n140\t * A ledger row this module has validated. Anything reaching a caller has crossed\n141\t * the read boundary below, so a caller may trust the shape and nothing else.\n142\t */\n143\texport interface LedgerRecord {\n144\t\trequestId: RequestId;\n145\t\tstatus: AnswerAttemptStatus;\n146\t\tquestionId: string | null;\n147\t\tdeviceId: string | null;\n148\t\tsurface: \"phone\" | \"watch\" | null;\n149\t\tleaseId: string | null;\n150\t\tstartedAtMs: EpochMs | null;\n151\t\tcreatedAtMs: EpochMs;\n152\t\tresolvedAtMs: EpochMs | null;\n153\t\tfailureCode: LedgerFailureCode | null;\n154\t\tguardsPassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n155\t\tcoverageEpoch: string;\n156\t}\n157\t\n158\t/** What `claimForAnswer` decided. Exhaustive on purpose. */\n159\texport type ClaimOutcome =\n160\t\t/** This request now owns an `in_flight` row. It MAY type. */\n161\t\t| { kind: \"claimed\"; coverageEpoch: string }\n162\t\t/** A row already existed: §11.4 replay. Return its recorded outcome, type nothing. */\n163\t\t| { kind: \"replay\"; record: LedgerRecord }\n164\t\t/**\n165\t\t * A status read already told a client nothing was received. Typing now would\n166\t\t * make that answer retroactively false, so it is REFUSED — permanently, not\n167\t\t * deferred.\n168\t\t */\n169\t\t| { kind: \"fenced\"; record: LedgerRecord };"}]},"uuid":"0de6c29a-d950-4816-ae16-931b12dd1c4a","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:31:32.484Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\attempt-ledger.ts","content":" * transaction, which a read-modify-rewrite of a JSON file structurally is not —\n * two processes, or one process and its own overtaken request, cannot be\n * serialised by rewriting a file.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHY SQLITE, AND WHAT IT BUYS BEYOND THE TRANSACTION\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * The file store's durability rested on an INFERENCE about NTFS: that a content\n * `fsync` publishes an earlier rename in the same directory, because both sit in\n * the volume metadata log. Microsoft documents `FlushFileBuffers` as flushing the\n * SPECIFIED FILE, and the documented durable-rename primitive\n * (`MoveFileEx(MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH)`) is one libuv never passes. The whole\n * witness mechanism existed to detect the consequence of that inference being\n * wrong, and could not detect the case where both files rolled back together.\n *\n * SQLite's durability is documented rather than inferred — see the `synchronous`\n * assertion below, which is load-bearing and checked rather than assumed.\n *\n * A second, quieter win: the JSON file was validated as a WHOLE, so ONE\n * incoherent record failed the file schema and quarantined every other record's\n * 24 hours with it. Rows are validated individually here, so a corrupt row costs\n * exactly one `requestId` its status.\n *\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO\n * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n * It does not decide what the CLIENT renders. It returns discriminated outcomes\n * and the honest-state discipline stays where it was: the five statuses mean\n * exactly what §11.5 says, nothing collapses into a vague \"sent\", and a state\n * this module cannot prove is never reported as terminal.\n */\n\nimport { and, eq, sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\nimport type { AnswerAttemptStatus, HostDb } from \"../db\";\nimport { answerAttempts, answerCoverageEpoch } from \"../db\";\nimport { LOG_PREFIX } from \"./config\";\nimport {\n\tassertDurableSqlite,\n\tbase64UrlEncode,\n\tisCanonicalWireId,\n\trandomBytes,\n} from \"./crypto\";\nimport type {\n\tAnswerGuardName,\n\tEpochMs,\n\tRequestId,\n\tSealedErrorCode,\n} from \"./types\";\nimport { ANSWER_GUARD_NAMES } from \"./types\";\n\n/** Bytes of randomness in a coverage epoch. 16 matches the install generation. */\nconst COVERAGE_EPOCH_BYTES = 16;\n\n/** The single-row epoch table's only key. */\nconst EPOCH_ROW_ID = 1;\n\n/**\n * Every failure code the ledger may PERSIST, and the set the read boundary\n * enforces.\n *\n * Wider than the old JSON store's two codes (`guard_failed`, `internal`) on\n * purpose. Since the claim moved to the top of `handleAnswer`, a request can be\n * refused for a reason the store never used to see — the panic write-disable, a\n * stale question, a lost lease, an unusable agent binding — and §11.4 says a replay\n * returns the RECORDED outcome. Recording `internal` for a `write_disabled`\n * refusal would make that replay lie about why, so the real code is stored and\n * rethrown verbatim.\n *\n * Every member is a §10 sealed code, so a stored value is always something a client\n * already knows how to render.\n */\nconst LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES = [\n\t\"stale_question\",\n\t\"already_resolved\",\n\t\"request_closed\",\n\t\"lease_held\",\n\t\"guard_failed\",\n\t\"picker_open\",\n\t\"capability_unsupported\",\n\t\"write_disabled\",\n\t\"bad_request\",\n\t\"internal\",\n] as const satisfies readonly SealedErrorCode[];\n\n/** A code the ledger can store, narrowed from §10's sealed set. */\nexport type LedgerFailureCode = (typeof LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES)[number];\n\n/**\n * The query surface this module uses, which BOTH the database and a transaction\n * satisfy.\n *\n * Deliberately structural rather than `tx as HostDb`: a transaction genuinely is\n * not a `HostDb` — it has no `$client` — and casting one to the other would have\n * been a lie that compiled. Every helper below takes this, so the same code runs\n * inside and outside a transaction and cannot accidentally reach for a\n * connection-level method (like the `synchronous` pragma) from inside one.\n */\ntype Queryable = Pick<HostDb, \"select\" | \"insert\" | \"update\" | \"delete\">;\n\n/**\n * A ledger row this module has validated. Anything reaching a caller has crossed\n * the read boundary below, so a caller may trust the shape and nothing else.\n */\nexport interface LedgerRecord {\n\trequestId: RequestId;\n\tstatus: AnswerAttemptStatus;\n\tquestionId: string | null;\n\tdeviceId: string | null;\n\tsurface: \"phone\" | \"watch\" | null;\n\tleaseId: string | null;\n\tstartedAtMs: EpochMs | null;\n\tcreatedAtMs: EpochMs;\n\tresolvedAtMs: EpochMs | null;\n\tfailureCode: LedgerFailureCode | null;\n\tguardsPassed: AnswerGuardName[];\n\tcoverageEpoch: string;\n}\n\n/** What `claimForAnswer` decided. Exhaustive on purpose. */\nexport type ClaimOutcome =\n\t/** This request now owns an `in_flight` row. It MAY type. */\n\t| { kind: \"claimed\"; coverageEpoch: string }\n\t/** A row already existed: §11.4 replay. Return its recorded outcome, type nothing. */\n\t| { kind: \"replay\"; record: LedgerRecord }\n\t/**\n\t * A status read already told a client nothing was received. Typing now would\n\t * make that answer retroactively false, so it is REFUSED — permanently, not\n\t * deferred.\n\t */\n\t| { kind: \"fenced\"; record: LedgerRecord };","numLines":130,"startLine":40,"totalLines":828}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9f3669f5-2d8e-4c29-bd92-b7f8c9b03882","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
