ts:220-225`. Opening a pairing window would persist its single-use pairing code in `host-service.log`.\n\nManual DB/key insertion would bypass the pairing boundary and is not acceptable. No device state was changed.\n\nSafe prerequisite: replace that log payload with `pairingRef` and `expiresAtMs`, ship/install the fix, then pair a fresh emulator identity through the authenticated local tRPC call and real `/pair/kex` → `/pair/confirm` exchange. Until then, do not reuse the current scratch emulator—it uses the physical phone identity and can perturb that phone’s negotiated capability session.","prompt":"Investigate and, if safe, create a distinct paired emulator identity for companion E2E without touching the physical phone identity/session. This is scratch/test setup, but pairing/device registration is security-sensitive: inspect existing pairing APIs/scripts and determine whether a separate local test device can be created through the real pairing boundary without UI. Do NOT copy or output keys/tokens/JWTs/device secrets. Do not revoke or mutate existing physical phone/watch identities. If creation would bypass the pairing boundary or require changing production state in an unsafe way, stop and report exact next step instead. Any scratch files only under tmp/phone-emu. Return whether achieved and safe usage instructions.","result":"Task: investigate a separate paired emulator identity without touching physical-device sessions.\n\nNot created. The real production pairing path currently logs the full secret-bearing QR URI at `packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts:1246-1249`, contradicting its own no-persistence contract at `packages/host-service/src/companion/pairing.ts:220-225`. Opening a pairing window would persist its single-use pairing code in `host-service.log`.\n\nManual DB/key insertion would bypass the pairing boundary and is not acceptable. No device state was changed.\n\nSafe prerequisite: replace that log payload with `pairingRef` and `expiresAtMs`, ship/install the fix, then pair a fresh emulator identity through the authenticated local tRPC call and real `/pair/kex` → `/pair/confirm` exchange. Until then, do not reuse the current scratch emulator—it uses the physical phone identity and can perturb that phone’s negotiated capability session.","isRawTranscript":false}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dcf8d381-8574-49fa-b255-790f87c093d8","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"855e12fc-b6cb-4edc-b32f-2069c489e5c2","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<agent-message from=\"emulator-identity\">\nTask: distinct paired emulator identity without touching physical devices. NOT created: production `openPairing()` currently logs the entire secret-bearing QR URI at `packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts:1246-1249` (`{ qrUri: handle.qrUri, ... }`), directly violating the no-secret-logging rule documented in `pairing.ts:220-225`. Calling authenticated local tRPC `companion.openPairing` would therefore persist the 120-second pairing code in host-service.log. Manual DB/key insertion would bypass the real pairing boundary and is not acceptable. Safe next step: production fix that log to `{ pairingRef: handle.pairingRef, expiresAtMs }`, run required simplify/review/build/install workflow, then pair a fresh emulator device through authenticated local tRPC + real `/pair/kex` and `/pair/confirm`; store only the client-side derived key in protected scratch and use its new deviceId in E2E. Until then, do not run `safe-e2e.ts`/`safe-freetext-e2e.ts` because they reuse the physical phone identity and perturb its capability session. No existing device state was changed.\n</agent-message>","commandMode":"prompt","origin":{"kind":"peer","from":"emulator-identity","senderTaskId":"aabce51de683bb79c","name":"emulator-identity","body":"Task: distinct paired emulator identity without touching physical devices. NOT created: production `openPairing()` currently logs the entire secret-bearing QR URI at `packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts:1246-1249` (`{ qrUri: handle.qrUri, ... }`), directly violating the no-secret-logging rule documented in `pairing.ts:220-225`. Calling authenticated local tRPC `companion.openPairing` would therefore persist the 120-second pairing code in host-service.log. Manual DB/key insertion would bypass the real pairing boundary and is not acceptable. Safe next step: production fix that log to `{ pairingRef: handle.pairingRef, expiresAtMs }`, run required simplify/review/build/install workflow, then pair a fresh emulator device through authenticated local tRPC + real `/pair/kex` and `/pair/confirm`; store only the client-side derived key in protected scratch and use its new deviceId in E2E. Until then, do not run `safe-e2e.ts`/`safe-freetext-e2e.ts` because they reuse the physical phone identity and perturb its capability session. No existing device state was changed."},"timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:36:40.382Z","isMeta":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e1786935-e25f-4b15-b275-393f743fd845","timestamp":"2026-08-12T02:36:40.382Z","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
