{"parentUuid":"c0161b09-4ce5-41e1-ad85-37a5ff15f6a1","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<agent-message from=\"byte-contract\">\nRoot cause traced. One-question freetext encodes 3 discrete frames: [digit N+1, body, CR] (`keystrokes.ts:1206-1249`). Current `injectSequence` sends each immediately after daemon `input-ok` (`answer.ts:1374-1452`). But daemon ACK is emitted synchronously as soon as `session.pty.write(Buffer)` returns (`pty-daemon/.../handlers.ts:120-133`); this proves OS/PTY acceptance, NOT that Claude consumed the digit and switched into inline-editor state. The user's ledger resolved all 3 frames in 5 ms, so body+CR can arrive before the TUI processes the editor-open digit; this exactly permits confirmed PTY writes while the picker remains open. Strong historical evidence: guarded code before commit 498be7a43 explicitly waited for screen advance between frames (25ms polling, up to 1s) and documented that this proved TUI consumption. 498be7a43 removed both guards AND that pacing as if it were only a veto, leaving no consumption barrier. 2.1.228 changelog contains no picker input contract change; version drift is less likely than this deterministic race.\nPrecise live proof: fresh innocuous one-question freetext pickers, drive identical [open, fixed body, CR] at controlled inter-frame delays: 0 ms, 25, 50, 100, 250 ms, at least 3 runs each. Observe only picker-open boolean / tool_result-present boolean and no content. Expect 0ms failure and delayed success threshold. Better production design: retain zero screen/transcript vetoes, but add a non-vetoing deterministic inter-frame pacing delay after every nonfinal frame (start conservatively 100ms, live-prove minimum), independent of renderer/display/lock. ACK stays the write/durability criterion; pacing is sequencing, never confirmation. Stronger but larger option: daemon `input-drain` cannot prove app consumption either, so no need. Also v2 whole-sequence blob is inherently race-prone for multi-frame answers and should remain unconfirmed but still needs paced protocol support or documented limitation.\n</agent-message>","commandMode":"prompt","origin":{"kind":"peer","from":"byte-contract","senderTaskId":"aa449e90c152aad03","name":"byte-contract","body":"Root cause traced. One-question freetext encodes 3 discrete frames: [digit N+1, body, CR] (`keystrokes.ts:1206-1249`). Current `injectSequence` sends each immediately after daemon `input-ok` (`answer.ts:1374-1452`). But daemon ACK is emitted synchronously as soon as `session.pty.write(Buffer)` returns (`pty-daemon/.../handlers.ts:120-133`); this proves OS/PTY acceptance, NOT that Claude consumed the digit and switched into inline-editor state. The user's ledger resolved all 3 frames in 5 ms, so body+CR can arrive before the TUI processes the editor-open digit; this exactly permits confirmed PTY writes while the picker remains open. Strong historical evidence: guarded code before commit 498be7a43 explicitly waited for screen advance between frames (25ms polling, up to 1s) and documented that this proved TUI consumption. 498be7a43 removed both guards AND that pacing as if it were only a veto, leaving no consumption barrier. 2.1.228 changelog contains no picker input contract change; version drift is less likely than this deterministic race.\nPrecise live proof: fresh innocuous one-question freetext pickers, drive identical [open, fixed body, CR] at controlled inter-frame delays: 0 ms, 25, 50, 100, 250 ms, at least 3 runs each. Observe only picker-open boolean / tool_result-present boolean and no content. Expect 0ms failure and delayed success threshold. Better production design: retain zero screen/transcript vetoes, but add a non-vetoing deterministic inter-frame pacing delay after every nonfinal frame (start conservatively 100ms, live-prove minimum), independent of renderer/display/lock. ACK stays the write/durability criterion; pacing is sequencing, never confirmation. Stronger but larger option: daemon `input-drain` cannot prove app consumption either, so no need. Also v2 whole-sequence blob is inherently race-prone for multi-frame answers and should remain unconfirmed but still needs paced protocol support or documented limitation."},"timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:17:13.723Z","isMeta":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"eb0f2a96-a62f-4d11-bacf-09acfd40a574","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:17:13.723Z","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
