{"parentUuid":"21bd21f8-b948-45e3-bc18-9065dff7de82","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_OfV6r4aHtpgeqVXhMPfKVKRG","type":"tool_result","content":"150\texport const notificationsRouter = router({\n151\t\t/**\n152\t\t * Agent lifecycle hook. The shell hook POSTs here; we normalize, resolve\n153\t\t * the terminal's workspace, and fan out over the WS event bus.\n154\t\t *\n155\t\t * Intentionally unauthenticated: a caller can only trigger a chime, a\n156\t\t * sidebar indicator, and the idempotent forward-only \"linked task →\n157\t\t * In Progress\" nudge for a real workspace. Reusing the host-service PSK\n158\t\t * would leak it into every agent shell's env for zero practical gain.\n159\t\t *\n160\t\t * (COMPANION-CAPTURE) That threat model now also covers a forged question:\n161\t\t * anything on localhost can POST a `companionQuestion` and make a phone\n162\t\t * display text it wrote. It CANNOT make the bridge type anything into a\n163\t\t * terminal — PROTOCOL.md §11.3 keeps the load-bearing answer guards on the\n164\t\t * transcript (guard 1) and a live screen snapshot (guard 5), both outside\n165\t\t * this endpoint's reach, precisely because this endpoint is unauthenticated.\n166\t\t * A forged capture therefore fails guard 1/5 and writes nothing. Do not\n167\t\t * promote anything sourced here into an answer precondition.\n168\t\t *\n169\t\t * \"Outside this endpoint's reach\" is mechanical, not aspirational, and both\n170\t\t * mechanisms were once missing. Guard 1 reads a transcript path DERIVED from\n171\t\t * host.db rather than the `transcriptPath` sent here, and requires the\n172\t\t * matching `tool_use` block to be positively observed; guard 5 enforces a\n173\t\t * minimum anchor length and a contiguous ascending row band, so the `header`\n174\t\t * and `label` strings sent here cannot be shrunk into a substring test any\n175\t\t * screen satisfies. Weakening either puts this endpoint back on the\n176\t\t * permitting path.\n177\t\t */\n178\t\thook: publicProcedure.input(hookInput).mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => {\n179\t\t\tconst eventType = mapEventType(input.eventType);\n180\t\t\tif (!eventType) {\n181\t\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(\n182\t\t\t\t\tinput,\n183\t\t\t\t\t`unmapped eventType ${String(input.eventType)}`,\n184\t\t\t\t);\n185\t\t\t\treturn {\n186\t\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n187\t\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n188\t\t\t\t\treason: \"unmapped-event-type\" as const,\n189\t\t\t\t};\n190\t\t\t}\n191\t\n192\t\t\tif (!input.terminalId) {\n193\t\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(input, \"no terminalId on the hook payload\");\n194\t\t\t\treturn {\n195\t\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n196\t\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n197\t\t\t\t\treason: \"no-terminal-id\" as const,\n198\t\t\t\t};\n199\t\t\t}\n200\t\n201\t\t\t// (DISPOSE-LIMBO) Three outcomes, not two. \"No row\" and \"row whose\n202\t\t\t// originWorkspaceId went NULL\" were one silent drop, so an agent POSTing\n203\t\t\t// against a terminal this host has never heard of looked exactly like the\n204\t\t\t// benign post-workspace-delete FK set-null case, and neither left a trace\n205\t\t\t// loud enough to notice. A missing row is an INVARIANT VIOLATION — the\n206\t\t\t// hook's env was stamped by a terminal we own, so the row must exist —\n207\t\t\t// and it is how a dispose that never completed surfaces here.\n208\t\t\t//\n209\t\t\t// All three still answer HTTP 200: the bash hook template treats a\n210\t\t\t// non-2xx as \"host-service declined\" and falls through to the legacy\n211\t\t\t// Electron hook path, so failing loudly on the wire would silently change\n212\t\t\t// agent behaviour. The distinct `reason` is the loud channel instead —\n213\t\t\t// (DISPOSE-LIMBO) makes both hook writers log the response BODY.\n214\t\t\t//\n215\t\t\t// We deliberately do NOT insert or adopt a row from a hook event. A row\n216\t\t\t// asserts \"an attachable PTY exists for this id\"; minting one from\n217\t\t\t// hearsay would let a later attach respawn a PTY for a terminal the user\n218\t\t\t// killed, and would resurrect exactly the rows the reaper is trying to\n219\t\t\t// finish killing.\n220\t\t\tconst terminalSession = ctx.db.query.terminalSessions\n221\t\t\t\t.findFirst({\n222\t\t\t\t\twhere: eq(terminalSessions.id, input.terminalId),\n223\t\t\t\t\tcolumns: { originWorkspaceId: true },\n224\t\t\t\t})\n225\t\t\t\t.sync();\n226\t\n227\t\t\tif (!terminalSession) {\n228\t\t\t\treportUnknownTerminal({\n229\t\t\t\t\tterminalId: input.terminalId,\n230\t\t\t\t\teventType: input.eventType,\n231\t\t\t\t\tmappedEventType: eventType,\n232\t\t\t\t\tagentId: input.agent?.agentId,\n233\t\t\t\t\tagentSessionId: input.agent?.sessionId,\n234\t\t\t\t});\n235\t\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(\n236\t\t\t\t\tinput,\n237\t\t\t\t\t`terminal ${input.terminalId} has no session row`,\n238\t\t\t\t);\n239\t\t\t\treturn {\n240\t\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n241\t\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n242\t\t\t\t\treason: \"unknown-terminal\" as const,\n243\t\t\t\t};\n244\t\t\t}\n245\t\n246\t\t\tif (!terminalSession.originWorkspaceId) {\n247\t\t\t\t// Benign and expected: deleting a workspace sets this FK to NULL while\n248\t\t\t\t// the agent in the terminal is still alive and still hooking.\n249\t\t\t\tconsole.warn("}]},"uuid":"737a1292-1617-4bab-b422-66ab886d8347","timestamp":"2026-08-12T11:22:42.381Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\trpc\\router\\notifications\\notifications.ts","content":"export const notificationsRouter = router({\n\t/**\n\t * Agent lifecycle hook. The shell hook POSTs here; we normalize, resolve\n\t * the terminal's workspace, and fan out over the WS event bus.\n\t *\n\t * Intentionally unauthenticated: a caller can only trigger a chime, a\n\t * sidebar indicator, and the idempotent forward-only \"linked task →\n\t * In Progress\" nudge for a real workspace. Reusing the host-service PSK\n\t * would leak it into every agent shell's env for zero practical gain.\n\t *\n\t * (COMPANION-CAPTURE) That threat model now also covers a forged question:\n\t * anything on localhost can POST a `companionQuestion` and make a phone\n\t * display text it wrote. It CANNOT make the bridge type anything into a\n\t * terminal — PROTOCOL.md §11.3 keeps the load-bearing answer guards on the\n\t * transcript (guard 1) and a live screen snapshot (guard 5), both outside\n\t * this endpoint's reach, precisely because this endpoint is unauthenticated.\n\t * A forged capture therefore fails guard 1/5 and writes nothing. Do not\n\t * promote anything sourced here into an answer precondition.\n\t *\n\t * \"Outside this endpoint's reach\" is mechanical, not aspirational, and both\n\t * mechanisms were once missing. Guard 1 reads a transcript path DERIVED from\n\t * host.db rather than the `transcriptPath` sent here, and requires the\n\t * matching `tool_use` block to be positively observed; guard 5 enforces a\n\t * minimum anchor length and a contiguous ascending row band, so the `header`\n\t * and `label` strings sent here cannot be shrunk into a substring test any\n\t * screen satisfies. Weakening either puts this endpoint back on the\n\t * permitting path.\n\t */\n\thook: publicProcedure.input(hookInput).mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => {\n\t\tconst eventType = mapEventType(input.eventType);\n\t\tif (!eventType) {\n\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(\n\t\t\t\tinput,\n\t\t\t\t`unmapped eventType ${String(input.eventType)}`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t\treturn {\n\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n\t\t\t\treason: \"unmapped-event-type\" as const,\n\t\t\t};\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tif (!input.terminalId) {\n\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(input, \"no terminalId on the hook payload\");\n\t\t\treturn {\n\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n\t\t\t\treason: \"no-terminal-id\" as const,\n\t\t\t};\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// (DISPOSE-LIMBO) Three outcomes, not two. \"No row\" and \"row whose\n\t\t// originWorkspaceId went NULL\" were one silent drop, so an agent POSTing\n\t\t// against a terminal this host has never heard of looked exactly like the\n\t\t// benign post-workspace-delete FK set-null case, and neither left a trace\n\t\t// loud enough to notice. A missing row is an INVARIANT VIOLATION — the\n\t\t// hook's env was stamped by a terminal we own, so the row must exist —\n\t\t// and it is how a dispose that never completed surfaces here.\n\t\t//\n\t\t// All three still answer HTTP 200: the bash hook template treats a\n\t\t// non-2xx as \"host-service declined\" and falls through to the legacy\n\t\t// Electron hook path, so failing loudly on the wire would silently change\n\t\t// agent behaviour. The distinct `reason` is the loud channel instead —\n\t\t// (DISPOSE-LIMBO) makes both hook writers log the response BODY.\n\t\t//\n\t\t// We deliberately do NOT insert or adopt a row from a hook event. A row\n\t\t// asserts \"an attachable PTY exists for this id\"; minting one from\n\t\t// hearsay would let a later attach respawn a PTY for a terminal the user\n\t\t// killed, and would resurrect exactly the rows the reaper is trying to\n\t\t// finish killing.\n\t\tconst terminalSession = ctx.db.query.terminalSessions\n\t\t\t.findFirst({\n\t\t\t\twhere: eq(terminalSessions.id, input.terminalId),\n\t\t\t\tcolumns: { originWorkspaceId: true },\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t\t.sync();\n\n\t\tif (!terminalSession) {\n\t\t\treportUnknownTerminal({\n\t\t\t\tterminalId: input.terminalId,\n\t\t\t\teventType: input.eventType,\n\t\t\t\tmappedEventType: eventType,\n\t\t\t\tagentId: input.agent?.agentId,\n\t\t\t\tagentSessionId: input.agent?.sessionId,\n\t\t\t});\n\t\t\twarnDroppedCompanionCapture(\n\t\t\t\tinput,\n\t\t\t\t`terminal ${input.terminalId} has no session row`,\n\t\t\t);\n\t\t\treturn {\n\t\t\t\tsuccess: true,\n\t\t\t\tignored: true as const,\n\t\t\t\treason: \"unknown-terminal\" as const,\n\t\t\t};\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tif (!terminalSession.originWorkspaceId) {\n\t\t\t// Benign and expected: deleting a workspace sets this FK to NULL while\n\t\t\t// the agent in the terminal is still alive and still hooking.\n\t\t\tconsole.warn(","numLines":100,"startLine":150,"totalLines":362}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"21bd21f8-b948-45e3-bc18-9065dff7de82","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
