{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T12:49:14.268Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0809c2ab0dc87af6</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_oOcqcVrWGRHBUbbVxXqfEr5U</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a0809c2ab0dc87af6.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify review complexity\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Target note:** worktree has zero uncommitted changes (`git status` clean). Reviewed the authored content of HEAD `25fcc9e93` \"merge upstream desktop-v1.20.2\" — the combined (`diff-tree --cc`) hunks, i.e. lines differing from BOTH parents = the fork's conflict resolutions/adaptations. 23 files, ~1.6k lines. If the intended target was something else, say so.\n\nNo edits made. Findings, worst cost first:\n\n**1. `apps/desktop/src/renderer/stores/destroy-workspace-intent.ts` (whole file, new)** — verbatim clone of `delete-workspace-intent.ts`. Lines 29-53 are character-identical to that file's 19-43 modulo `Delete`→`Destroy`. Simpler: one `createWorkspaceIntentStore()` factory (~25 lines, both stores call it), or one store with `mode: \"soft\" | \"destroy\"`. Cost: every upstream change to the delete-intent shape (upstream owns that file and just reshaped `close`/`setOpen` to be id-scoped in this very merge) must be hand-mirrored into the fork clone, with no compiler link between them — silent drift.\n\n**2. `apps/desktop/src/renderer/stores/delete-workspace-intent.ts:21,23,30-35`** — `open` and `setOpen` are dead in the fork. Grepped all consumers: `DeleteWorkspaceMount.tsx:23-24` reads only `target` and `close`; the other 3 callers only `.request()`. Simpler: `{target, request, close}`; `request` becomes `set({target})`. Cost: fork carries an upstream dialog-latching mechanism whose only consumer (the destroy dialog) now lives in a different store — reads as live state, is not.\n\n**3. `packages/host-service/src/trpc/router/project/handlers.ts:33-40` ↔ `project.ts:65-72`** — `REPO_PATHS_CASE_INSENSITIVE` + `repoPathMatches` duplicated verbatim. The handlers.ts comment justifies it as \"importing it back would close a cycle\" — true only for a direct import; `project.ts` imports `./handlers` and both already import `./utils/*`. Simpler: `utils/repo-path-match.ts`. Cost: the `(PATH-CI-DEDUPE)` invariant (SQLite BINARY equality misses case-variant paths on win32/darwin) now has two definitions; fixing one leaves the other, across 4 call sites (project.ts:402,433,816 + handlers.ts:187).\n\n**4. `packages/host-service/src/trpc/router/project/project.ts:404-417` vs `444-458`** — two near-identical `local-path` candidate literals in one procedure, already divergent: name fallback is `repoName ?? basename` in the non-git branch vs `name || repoName || basename` in the git branch (a renamed non-git project reports its stale repoName), and only the first carries `satisfies FindByPathCandidate`. Simpler: `toLocalCandidate(row, fallbackName, matchesExpected)`. Cost: adding a field to `FindByPathCandidate` requires finding both literals, and only one is type-anchored.\n\n**5. `packages/host-service/src/trpc/router/workspace-cleanup/workspace-cleanup.ts:294-338`** — the merge moved preflight inside the archive `try`, reaching 6 nesting levels (fn → try → if → else → try → if at :314). Two simplifications: (a) the multi-repo half is already a helper (`preflightMultiRepoDirtyCheck`, `multi-repo-cleanup.ts:75`) while the single-repo half stays 33 lines inline — extract the symmetric helper and the block collapses to a 4-line if/else; (b) the inner `try/catch` at :305-336 catches the `TRPCError` it threw itself and re-throws via `if (err instanceof TRPCError) throw err` — wrapping only the two awaits and checking `state.hasChanges` after removes that dance entirely. Cost: this exact block re-indented wholesale in this merge (it *was* the conflict); any future upstream reorder of the destroy steps re-conflicts all 33 lines instead of one call.\n\n**6. `apps/desktop/.../DashboardSidebarWorkspaceItem/DashboardSidebarWorkspaceItem.tsx:261-303` vs `384-426`** — `DashboardSidebarWorkspaceContextMenu` rendered twice with an identical 25-prop list (differs only in prop ordering and `content` vs `expandedContent`). Simpler: build the props object once, spread at both sites, vary children. Cost: this merge had to edit `onDeletePermanently` in both places; the fork already duplicates `onSnooze/onUnsnooze/onArchive/onUnarchive/onRestore/sectionState` there — a menu prop added to one site only fails silently in the collapsed-or-expanded state the developer didn't test.\n\n**7. Same invariant restated 4×, ~55 lines of prose** — \"archive-first destroy tombstones the row at step 0 → a row-local dialog unmounts mid-destroy → the teardown-failure force-retry pane is lost\": `destroy-workspace-intent.ts:8-28`, `DestroyWorkspaceMount.tsx:5-20`, `useDashboardSidebarWorkspaceItemActions.ts:133-141`, `KanbanCard.tsx:318-322`. Simpler: state once in the store, one-line pointers elsewhere. Cost: four independently-drifting copies of one upstream-owned fact; two of the four were already being rewritten in this merge.\n\n**8. `useDashboardSidebarWorkspaceItemActions.ts:145`** — `if (isMainWorkspace) return;` is unreachable: `onDeletePermanently` is wired only when `sectionState === \"deleted\"` (DashboardSidebarWorkspaceItem.tsx:291-292, 414-415), and the fork's `deleteWorkspace` no-ops mains so a main never enters the bin. Its own comment concedes the old dialog \"was simply never mounted for one\". Simpler: delete the guard. Cost: a silent no-op branch that will never be exercised, so it can't be trusted or tested — and it contradicts the repo's fail-loud rule if it ever *is* reached.\n\n**9. Two divergent constructions of the destroy request payload** — `useDashboardSidebarWorkspaceItemActions.ts:146-149` uses `workspaceName || branch`; `KanbanCard.tsx:324-327` uses `workspace.name` with no fallback. Simpler: one `requestDestroy(workspace)` helper next to the store. Cost: a nameless workspace shows an empty name in the destroy dialog from the kanban path only.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>123770</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>33</tool_uses><duration_ms>395732</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
