ng real QuestionStore in answer harness—apply only if contained and safe, otherwise explicitly skip; (D) findQuestionFence .all/loop inefficiency and altitude suggestion to fold question ownership into claim. IMPORTANT: do not add/edit generated drizzle migration files; moving to transactional claim may be correctness-sensitive and outside cleanup, so skip if it changes behavior or requires schema/index migration. Any index requires user-run drizzle generate and should be skipped for this batch. Preserve the current durable fresh-request fence and all tests. Run focused tests and scoped Biome after edits. Do not commit. Report applied/skipped with reasons.","outputFile":"C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a2b4019afbe72f0d4.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f8cd5290-f683-4783-8a35-75fefd881a4f","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\tmp\\installed-asar-check","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T12:50:25.667Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a748be3dad8402c62</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_0sRA7swaWWDvm0vbN5Dcqia2</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a748be3dad8402c62.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Simplify review altitude\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Preface: diff recovery\n\nWorktree assigned to me was clean — the uncommitted work lives in the shared checkout, which git tooling refuses to touch from an isolated worktree. Reconstructed the diff by content-comparing shared checkout vs. this worktree at the same commit (`25fcc9e93`), ignoring CR (shared checkout is CRLF). Recovered set = 9 files: `answer.ts`, `attempt-ledger.ts`, `question-store.ts`, `companion/index.ts`, `terminal/terminal.ts`, `notifications.ts`, `FEATURES.md`, + 2 tests. Diffs saved under `&lt;worktree&gt;/tmp/altitude/`. No edits made.\n\n## Altitude findings\n\n**1. Question-scoped fence sits inside a validator, one layer below the fence protocol that already exists**\n`packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts:774-805` (`requireCurrentPendingAnswerQuestion`) + `attempt-ledger.ts:~744-765` (`findQuestionFence`)\nA third fencing mechanism (question-keyed, scan, thrown `SealedError`) added beside the existing request-keyed CAS protocol (`claimForAnswer` → `claimed`/`replay`/`fenced`, transactional). It runs *after* the claim, so it must exclude its own just-written `in_flight` row — correctness depends on a side effect performed ~250 lines earlier in a different subsystem.\nCost: two fences, two keys, two error vocabularies to keep in sync; any future entry point that claims without the validator (or validates without claiming) gets a different verdict. Worse, the throw goes through `catch (error) { if (error instanceof SealedError) throw error; }` (answer.ts:~1241) which records no outcome — the request's own `in_flight` row survives and then fences *every* later request for that question permanently. Generalization: fold the question predicate into `claimForAnswer`'s transaction as a new `ClaimOutcome` member; no self-exclusion, no orphan row, one place decides \"may this request type\".\n\n**2. Ledger status set hardcoded as a raw SQL string — three copies, none type-checked**\n`attempt-ledger.ts:752` — `` sql`${answerAttempts.status} IN ('in_flight', 'confirmed', 'unconfirmed')` ``\nThe authoritative union is `answerAttemptStatuses` (`db/schema.ts:327-333`, deliberately declared table-side). Copy 2 is the test fake (`answer-guardless.test.ts` `memoryLedger`, `||` chain). Module imports only `and, eq, sql`; the only pre-existing `` sql`` `` uses are arithmetic increments (`rotations + 1`) — raw predicate SQL is new here, and `751` reimplements `ne()` too.\nCost: adding a 6th status compiles clean and silently falls outside the fence → the exact double-type this change exists to prevent. Fix: `export const FENCING_ATTEMPT_STATUSES = answerAttemptStatuses.filter(...)` (or explicit `satisfies readonly AnswerAttemptStatus[]`), `inArray()` + `ne()`, and import the same const into the test fake.\n\n**3. `findQuestionFence` conflates \"validate all candidates\" with \"return the first\"**\n`attempt-ledger.ts:~756-763`\nSelects every matching row (no `ORDER BY`, no `limit`), then loops and `return`s on iteration 1. The \"refusing to type past unreadable durable state\" throw only fires if the *unvalidatable* row happens to be first in an unordered result.\nCost: the safety property is nondeterministic — same DB, different SQLite plan, different verdict. Either validate the whole set before choosing, or `.limit(1)` with an explicit deterministic order and own that the guarantee is now \"the newest fence row\".\n\n**4. \"Remote provenance wins\" written twice, in opposite directions**\n`question-store.ts:1958-1959` (`resolve`: `remoteAnsweredAtMs ?? atMs`) and `1971-1976` (`markRemoteAnswered`: overwrite `resolvedBy/resolvedAtMs` when already `resolved`)\nOne rule, two mirror-image implementations selected by arrival order, plus a parallel nullable field pair (`remoteAnswerBy`/`remoteAnsweredAtMs`) shadowing `resolvedBy`/`resolvedAtMs`.\nCost: a third settlement path (transcript reconcile, a future `settleFromToolResult`) must re-derive the same precedence or silently attribute a phone answer to the desk — and nothing fails if it forgets. Generalization: one `applyAnswerProvenance(question, {by, atMs})` holding the precedence once; settlement stamps state only.\n\n**5. `already_resolved` payload mixes in-memory and durable sources, preferring the weaker one**\n`answer.ts:789-790` — `resolvedBy: question.remoteAnswerBy`, `resolvedAtMs: question.remoteAnsweredAtMs ?? durableFence.resolvedAtMs`\nThe fence row is the durable authority and carries `deviceId` + `surface` + `resolvedAtMs`; the code reads memory first for one field, durable as fallback for the other. After a `stale` settle `markRemoteAnswered` returns false, so `remoteAnswerBy` stays null → clients get `outcome: \"answered\"` with `resolvedBy: null` for reasons unrelated to them.\nCost: reintroduces the memory/durable split the ledger exists to remove (repo rule: DB is source of truth); the null case is untestable from the client side. Build the whole payload from `durableFence`; if `deviceLabel` is genuinely needed on the wire, persist it on the ledger row rather than reaching into memory.\n\n**6. Best-effort cosmetic repaint promoted to a required dependency of the answer contract**\n`answer.ts:189-196` (`AnswerDeps.nudgeRepaint`), `answer.ts:299-303` (startup assert), `index.ts:1948` (wiring), `answer.ts:1164` (call)\nThe doc comment says it is \"never an answer precondition or outcome check, failure may only be logged\" — yet it is a mandatory `AnswerDeps` member with its own bespoke result union and a fail-loud startup check. It is companion-only (`writeAcknowledgedInputToSession` has exactly one composition root).\nCost: every deps constructor and test fake must now stub a no-op (3 sites added in this diff alone); `AnswerDeps` grows a member that carries no answer semantics. Lower-altitude-free alternative: hang it off the `RawPtyWriter` adapter that already owns the pty writes and already carries an asserted `writerKind` marker (`writeInput.repaint`) — zero new `AnswerDeps` surface, zero new assert, and `injectSequence` fires it at sequence end where the write abstraction already lives.\n\n**7. `assertAnswerDeps` grows one hand-written `typeof` branch per dependency**\n`answer.ts:297-313` — three near-identical branches now (`nudgeRepaint`, `writeFramed`, `snapshotScreen`), each with its own message.\nCost: the runtime guard is a manual restatement of a type that is only unsafe because the composition root hands over an `as unknown as AnswerDeps`. Adding a dep and forgetting the branch is silent. A `REQUIRED_DEP_FNS` table (key → reason) collapses it to one loop; a typed factory removes the need entirely.\n\n**8. `pty.resize` failure handled at two different depths with two idioms**\n`terminal.ts:1391-1401` (try/catch + `console.warn`, restore resize only) and `terminal.ts:1414-1433` (`nudgeTerminalSessionRepaint`, try/catch → `{error}`)\nThe *first* resize (`terminal.ts:1387`, the shrink — the one that actually pokes the PTY first) is unguarded. The companion wrapper catches it; the two other callers (`terminal.ts:1467` scheduled fallback, `terminal.ts:3442` forced attach nudge) do not.\nCost: a dead-PTY throw is absorbed for the phone path and propagates on the attach path — the reverse of the risk ranking. Make `nudgeRepaint` itself total (never throws, logs both resizes); the companion wrapper then reduces to the `writableSession` result passthrough and the other two callers inherit the protection.\n\n**9. Test fake reimplements the exact rule under test**\n`answer-guardless.test.ts` (`answerHarness`, `questions` fake ~196-236)\n`markRemoteAnswered`'s resolved-race precedence is copy-pasted from `question-store.ts` into a `as unknown as QuestionStore` fake — so \"keeps phone provenance when positive settlement wins the marking race\" asserts the fake's behaviour, not the store's. `memoryLedger` similarly re-encodes the fence status set.\nCost: change the precedence in `question-store.ts` and these stay green while the real thing regresses. The store is already pure in-memory — use `createQuestionStore` in the harness and delete the fake.\n\n**10. `markRemoteAnswered` has three failure channels; the production caller reads none**\n`question-store.ts:1964-1977`, called at `answer.ts:1156`\nThrows on unknown id, returns `false` on `stale`, returns `true` otherwise — return value discarded. The `resolve()` call it replaced *did* check its `false` and logged `resolve_skipped` (deleted in this diff).\nCost: the \"device provenance was not recorded\" case is now unobservable in logs; a reader can't tell whether the boolean is meaningful. Either consume it (log the stale skip) or make the method `void` and drop the branch.\n\n**11. `FEATURES.md` behaviour amendments appended into a single multi-thousand-character table cell**\n`FEATURES.md:78-79`\nTwo rows rewritten in place; the bridge row is one continuous line of prose that now also carries the repaint/settlement semantics.\nCost: nightly-merge conflicts on that line are all-or-nothing and unreviewable; the marker gate only checks token presence, so prose↔code drift is undetectable by design. Low priority (it is the established fork convention) but each amendment raises the cost of the next merge — per-marker subsections with the token as a heading would keep the gate working and make conflicts local.\n\n## Not flagged\n\n`notifications.ts:158-172` comment rewrite and `index.ts:1235-1245` pairing-log redaction (`qrUri` → `pairingRef`) are appropriately scoped — the log change removes a secret from diagnostics rather than adding a special case, and the comment now matches the guardless model.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>132620</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>47</tool_uses><duration_ms>473378</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
