{"parentUuid":"3d77fed9-b2f6-4842-8ac0-ae2e9ed1d756","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"44611460-2829-462e-b2e9-af8e653be0db","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_NIHznW9McRoUwZno9ExZfRDu","type":"tool_result","content":"825-\t//\n826-\t//    It also subsumes §11.4 replay. The claim is a compare-and-set, so a repeat\n827-\t//    of the SAME requestId does not re-execute — it comes back as `replay` with\n828-\t//    the recorded outcome, or as `fenced` if a status read got there first.\n829-\tconst claim = deps.ledger.claimForAnswer({\n830-\t\trequestId: request.requestId,\n831-\t\tquestionId: request.questionId,\n832-\t\tdeviceId: ctx.device.deviceId,\n833-\t\tsurface: request.surface,\n834-\t\tstartedAtMs: deps.now(),\n835-\t});\n836-\n837:\tif (claim.kind === \"fenced\") {\n838-\t\t// A status read already told a client nothing was received for this\n839-\t\t// requestId. Typing now would make that answer retroactively false, so this\n840-\t\t\\ is refused permanently rather than deferred. The client is told plainly:\n841-\t\t// the request was closed out, start a new one.\n842-\t\t// `request_closed`, NOT `already_resolved`. The latter means a question was\n843-\t\t// answered — by another device or at the desk — and a client renders it as\n844-\t\t// \"already answered\", which for a fenced request is false in the one direction\n845-\t\t\\ that matters: nothing was answered, this request was closed out. The chip\n846-\t\t// would have contradicted the very message explaining what happened. The\n847-\t\t\\ remedy differs too: `already_resolved` means stop, whereas this means the\n848-\t\t// question may still be open, so submit a NEW request.\n849-\t\tthrow sealed(\n850-\t\t\t409,\n851-\t\t\t\"request_closed\",\n852-\t\t\t\"a status read already reported this request as never received, and fenced it; it will never be typed. The question may still be open — submit a new answer.\",\n853-\t\t\tnull,\n854-\t\t);\n855-\t}\n856-\n857:\tif (claim.kind === \"replay\") {\n858-\t\tconst previous = claim.record;\n859-\t\tif (previous.status === \"failed\") {\n860-\t\t\t// The request-id fence stays closed, but legacy guard failures never\n861-\t\t\t// re-surface as a guard refusal. A fresh request may answer the still-open\n862-\t\t\t// question through the guardless path.\n863-\t\t\tthrow sealed(\n864-\t\t\t\t409,\n865-\t\t\t\t\"request_closed\",\n866-\t\t\t\t\"this request id already failed and is never re-executed; submit a new answer request\",\n867-\t\t\t);\n868-\t\t}\n869-\t\t// (ANSWER-INFLIGHT) The sequence for this very requestId is still typing.\n870-\t\t// Reporting `unconfirmed` here — which the client treats as terminal — for a\n871-\t\t// write about to confirm was the original sin this whole area is fixing.\n872:\t\tif (previous.status === \"claimed\" || previous.status === \"in_flight\") {\n873-\t\t\tthrow sealed(\n874-\t\t\t\t409,\n875-\t\t\t\t\"lease_held\",\n876-\t\t\t\t\"this answer is still being typed into the terminal; poll /v1/answer/status\",\n877-\t\t\t\t{ leaseHolderLabel: null, expiresInMs: null },\n878-\t\t\t);\n879-\t\t}\n880-\t\treturn ledgerRecordToResponse(previous);\n881-\t}\n882-\n883-\t// (LEDGER-REFUSAL) Every refusal from here on records an outcome before it\n884-\t// escapes.\n--\n1329-\t\t\tdeps.leases.release(lease.leaseId);\n1330-\t\t}\n1331-\t} catch (error) {\n1332-\t\t// A row still `in_flight` here means the throw got past every path that\n1333-\t\t// records its own outcome, so this is the last chance to stop it claiming\n1334-\t\t// forever that keystrokes are landing.\n1335-\t\t//\n1336-\t\t// A SealedError is a REFUSAL with a known reason, recorded verbatim so §11.4's\n1337-\t\t// replay can say why. Anything else is a fault of unknown extent — it could\n1338-\t\t// have escaped after bytes moved — so it degrades to `unconfirmed` rather than\n1339-\t\t// to a `failed` that would assert nothing was typed.\n1340-\t\tconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\n1341:\t\tif (durableStatus === \"claimed\" || durableStatus === \"in_flight\") {\n1342-\t\t\tdeps.ledger.recordOutcome({\n1343-\t\t\t\trequestId: request.requestId,\n1344-\t\t\t\t...(error instanceof SealedError\n1345-\t\t\t\t\t? {\n1346-\t\t\t\t\t\t\tstatus: \"failed\" as const,\n1347-\t\t\t\t\t\t\tfailureCode: error.body.code as AttemptFailureCode,\n1348-\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n1349-\t\t\t\t\t: { status: \"unconfirmed\" as const, failureCode: null }),\n1350-\t\t\t\tresolvedAtMs: null,\n1351-\t\t\t\tguardsPassed: [],\n1352-\t\t\t\tleaseId: null,\n1353-\t\t\t});\n--\n1615- * as a fifth one the client would have to learn.\n1616- */\n1617-function toWireOutcome(resolved: StatusOutcome): AnswerStatusOutcome {\n1618-\tif (resolved.kind === \"not_received\") {\n1619-\t\treturn { kind: \"not_received\" };\n1620-\t}\n1621-\tif (resolved.kind === \"unconfirmed\") {\n1622-\t\treturn { kind: \"unconfirmed\", why: resolved.why };\n1623-\t}\n1624-\tif (resolved.record.status === \"closed_not_received\") {\n1625-\t\treturn { kind: \"not_received\" };\n1626-\t}\n1627:\tif (resolved.record.status === \"claimed\") {\n1628-\t\treturn {\n1629-\t\t\tkind: \"known\",\n1630-\t\t\tstatus: \"in_flight\",\n1631-\t\t\tquestionId: resolved.record.questionId as QuestionId | null,\n1632-\t\t\tresolvedAtMs: null,\n1633-\t\t\tfailureCode: null,\n1634-\t\t};\n1635-\t}\n1636-\treturn {\n1637-\t\tkind: \"known\",\n1638-\t\tstatus: resolved.record.status,\n1639-\t\tquestionId: resolved.record.questionId as QuestionId | null,"}]},"uuid":"8773ad96-cd5d-4594-8377-56110fe2ab74","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:10:22.787Z","toolUseResult":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"825-\t//\n826-\t//    It also subsumes §11.4 replay. The claim is a compare-and-set, so a repeat\n827-\t//    of the SAME requestId does not re-execute — it comes back as `replay` with\n828-\t//    the recorded outcome, or as `fenced` if a status read got there first.\n829-\tconst claim = deps.ledger.cl