{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:42:01.398Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6ff688806d1aec1c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a6ff688806d1aec1c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review fix efficiency\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Efficiency review of shared-checkout uncommitted diff (5 modified + 1 new file, all `packages/host-service/src/companion/`).\n\n## Finding 1 — duplicate durable ledger read (real, only material one)\n\n`C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\answer.ts:1362-1364`\n\n```ts\nconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\nif (durableStatus === \"claimed\" || durableStatus === \"in_flight\") {\n    const durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);\n```\n\nTwo `ledger.get` calls, same `requestId`, same tick, no write between → second is strictly redundant.\n\nConcrete cost: `get` = `selectRow` (`.select()` all columns from `answer_attempts` `WHERE requestId=?` + drizzle row mapping) then `validate` (~10 field/cross-field checks). So one extra SQLite query + row materialization + validation per throw that reaches the outer catch with a `claimed`/`in_flight` row — i.e. every routine SealedError refusal (guard_failed, already_resolved, lease_held), not just rare faults.\n\nFix: read once, derive both.\n\n```ts\nconst durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);\nconst durableStatus = durable?.status;\n```\n\nNote the same path already re-reads at `answer.ts:1328` (inner catch) before `recordOutcome`; that one is not redundant (write intervenes), so the internal-error path legitimately costs 2 reads, 3 today.\n\n## Finding 2 — redundant store lookup + dead branch (low cost, cleanup)\n\n`...\\companion\\index.ts:1445-1467` (`markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish`)\n\n`deps.questions.get(questionId)` is called, then `questions.markRemoteAnswered` does its own `byId.get(questionId)` internally (`question-store.ts:1964-1965`). Both are in-memory `Map` gets → cost negligible (no I/O), so this is style not throughput.\n\nThe `if (question === null) return;` branch is dead as a guard: `markRemoteAnswered` already returns `false` for an unknown id, and the next line returns on `false`. Only `wasResolved` genuinely needs the pre-read. Collapse to reading `state` once:\n\n```ts\nconst wasResolved = deps.questions.get(questionId)?.state === \"resolved\";\nif (!deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)) return;\nif (!wasResolved) return;\n```\n\n## Checked, no issue\n\n- `toLedgerFailureCode` (`attempt-ledger.ts:134-138`): linear `includes` over a 10-element frozen literal, error paths only. Cheaper than a Set/object map at this size — leave it.\n- `answer.ts:1177-1193` try/catch around `markRemoteAnswered`: zero-cost on the non-throwing path.\n- `AnswerDeps.markRemoteAnswered` closure created once per `createAnswerDeps` (`index.ts:2046`), not per request.\n- Test changes (`answer-guardless.test.ts`, `attempt-ledger.test.ts`, new `answer-settlement-events.test.ts`): no sleeps, real timers, or per-test DB spin-up added; new settlement test is pure in-memory. No efficiency debt.\n\nNo edits made.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>62289</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>137525</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:43:31.618Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae444045e6510c1b4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\ae444045e6510c1b4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review fix simplicity\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Reviewed the uncommitted diff in the shared checkout (5 modified + 1 new file, all under `packages/host-service/src/companion/`). Biome clean. Quality findings only, no edits made.\n\n## Findings (ranked)\n\n**1. Doc block detached from its function — `index.ts:1430-1469`**\n`markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` was inserted *between* the docblock (\"Wraps the store's capture sink with the effects that must fire exactly when custody changes… ORDER MATTERS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS… `inner.capture` throws `CaptureRejectedError`…\") and `createNotifyingCaptureSink` at line 1471. That comment now documents the new function; the sink it describes has none.\nCost: IDE hover / any reader of `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` gets the sink's capture-order contract, which is false for it; the sink's real ordering invariant becomes invisible at its definition. Fix: move the block back above line 1471, give the new function its own.\n\n**2. New function's inverted gate is undocumented — `index.ts:1451-1459`**\n```ts\nconst wasResolved = question.state === \"resolved\";\nif (!deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)) return;\nif (!wasResolved) return;   // publish ONLY if it was ALREADY resolved\n```\nReads backwards without context (publish a resolution frame only when it was already resolved). Cost: in a file where comments are the spec, the one branch encoding the PostToolUse-race correction carries zero explanation → next editor \"fixes\" the inversion. Needs one line: the not-yet-resolved case is published by the sink's `resolve`, this path only re-publishes corrected remote provenance.\n\n**3. Duplicated `question.resolved` frame literal — `index.ts:1460-1468` vs `1514-1522`**\nSame 4-field frame constructed in two places. The file already has `publishPendingQuestion` (line 1781, exported and unit-tested) as the extraction precedent.\nCost: renderer wire-contract drift — a field added in one publisher silently missing from the other. Fix: `publishQuestionResolved(deps, { questionId, resolvedAtMs, resolvedBy })`.\n\n**4. Redundant ledger read + dead optional chain — `answer.ts:1362-1377`**\n```ts\nconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\nif (durableStatus === \"claimed\" || durableStatus === \"in_flight\") {\n    const durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);   // second read of the same row\n    ...\n    leaseId: durable?.leaseId ?? null,                    // row provably exists here\n}\n```\nCost: an extra SQLite query on every failing answer, plus a `?? null` fallback that can never fire yet reads as a real case. Fix: one `const durable = deps.ledger.get(...)`, branch on `durable?.status`, then `durable.leaseId`.\n\n**5. Stale comments contradicted by the lines directly under them — `answer.ts:1317-1320` and `1354-1361`**\nBoth paragraphs state the old unconditional rule — \"must not be downgraded to 'never sent'… Unknown outcome, stated as such\" and \"Anything else… degrades to `unconfirmed` rather than to a `failed` that would assert nothing was typed\" — while the new `preWrite` branches (1329-1344, 1365-1374) now record exactly `failed`/`internal` for a non-sealed fault when the row is still `claimed`.\nCost: the spec-comment and the code disagree at the highest-stakes point in the file (whether the wire says bytes may have landed). Highest-value cleanup after #1.\n\n**6. Same mapping spelled 4× / nested ternary — `answer.ts:1330-1345`, `1366-1374`**\n`preWrite ? \"failed\" : \"unconfirmed\"` + `preWrite ? \"internal\" : null` repeated across the `recordOutcome` and `appendAnswerAudit` calls, then re-derived as a 3-level nested ternary in the outer catch.\nCost: four independent edit sites for one rule → attempt row and audit row can diverge. Fix: one `const outcome = preWrite ? { status: \"failed\", failureCode: \"internal\" } as const : { status: \"unconfirmed\", failureCode: null } as const;` spread into both.\n\n**7. Membership check duplicated with two different casts — `attempt-ledger.ts:135` vs `:551`**\nNew `toLedgerFailureCode` does `(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly SealedErrorCode[]).includes(code) ? (code as LedgerFailureCode) : \"internal\"`; line 551 already does `(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly string[]).includes(row.failureCode)`. Also the only export in this heavily-documented file with no doc comment.\nCost: three casts where a single `function isLedgerFailureCode(c: string): c is LedgerFailureCode` would serve both sites cast-free at the narrow point.\n\n**8. Error convention split at the same call site — `answer.ts:1177-1193`**\nThe new `markRemoteAnswered` dep is wrapped in `try/catch` + log, while `nudgeRepaint` immediately below returns `{ error }` — two conventions for two adjacent post-write bookkeeping hooks. The dep also types the return as `void` although the underlying store method returns a boolean \"applied\" signal that is discarded at the boundary.\nCost: minor, but the swallowing `try/catch` in `answer.ts` is the kind of masking the repo bans elsewhere; moving it into the adapter lambda (`index.ts:2043-2050`) or matching `nudgeRepaint`'s result shape keeps `answer.ts` free of it.\n\n**9. Test harness borrows a method off a throwaway registry — `answer-guardless.test.ts:253-254`, `394`**\n`locks.runExclusive = options.lockRun` then, mid-test, `harness.deps.locks.runExclusive = createTerminalLockRegistry().runExclusive` — a second full registry allocated purely to steal one closure-bound method (whose own empty lock map is what the retry then uses).\nCost: the restore reads as \"reset the registry\" but is actually \"swap in a different registry's state.\" A `harness.restoreLocks()` helper (or a settable override slot on the harness) says what it does.\n\n**10. New test stubs diverge from the file-local precedent — `answer-settlement-events.test.ts:63-99`**\nThree separate `as never` casts (`push`, two `events`), and two distinct `events` stubs both pushing into the same `frames` array. `question-freshness.test.ts:169-193` already establishes one `as unknown as NotifyingSinkDeps` fixture factory for these deps.\nCost: low — style drift plus a reader wondering whether the two `events` objects are meaningfully different (they aren't).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>75020</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>226166</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
