imports `./index` to test 25 lines of domain logic, dragging push senders, HTTP routes, and host-db wiring into the test graph. Right altitude: `question-store.ts` (or a small `question-events.ts` beside it), where `markRemoteAnswered` itself lives (`question-store.ts:772,1964`).\n\n**A3 — Wiring type reused as a domain-function parameter type.** `index.ts:1446` — `deps: Pick&lt;NotifyingSinkDeps, \"questions\" | \"events\"&gt;`. `NotifyingSinkDeps` means \"what the capture-sink wrapper needs to be built\"; this fn is not a sink. Cost: test callers fabricate `as never` casts (`answer-settlement-events.test.ts:69,72,94`), and any rename/reshape of the sink dep bundle ripples into an unrelated function.\n\n**A4 — Two ways to mark the same thing on one deps object, at different altitudes.** `answer.ts:240` (`questions: QuestionStore`) vs `answer.ts:246` (new `markRemoteAnswered(...)`). The handler holds both the raw store (whose `markRemoteAnswered` does *not* publish) and the wrapped, publishing dep. Cost: a future edit writing `deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)` type-checks and silently drops the corrective frame — reintroducing exactly the bug this diff fixes. Already visible in the harness: `answer-guardless.test.ts:262` wires `markRemoteAnswered: questions.markRemoteAnswered`, i.e. the non-publishing variant, so the double diverges from prod by construction. Right altitude: hand `handleAnswer` a store already decorated with publishing (one object, one method) so the wrong call is unrepresentable.\n\n**A5 — Same policy, two idioms, 15 lines apart.** `answer.ts:1177-1193` (try/catch + log) vs `answer.ts:1194-1202` (`nudgeRepaint` returns `{error}` and is checked). Both are \"post-write bookkeeping must never fail a confirmed answer\". The guarantee is also asserted in prose on the dep at `answer.ts:242-245`. Cost: the never-throws contract is enforced at the call site inside a ~300-line handler instead of in the adapter (`index.ts:2048`), so a second caller must re-implement the swallow, and the handler's confirmed-happy-path gains 10 lines of noise. Cheaper: swallow+log inside the `createAnswerDeps` closure, keep `handleAnswer` declarative.\n\n**A6 — Throw→durable-outcome classification duplicated with divergent rules.** `answer.ts:1328-1345` vs `answer.ts:1362-1378`. Inner: `preWrite ? failed/internal : unconfirmed/null`. Outer: `preWrite || SealedError → failed`, code `preWrite ? \"internal\" : toLedgerFailureCode(...)`. Cost: two sites to keep in sync, and the divergence is invisible without side-by-side reading; a third catch path added later will pick one arbitrarily. Right altitude: one named helper (e.g. `classifyThrowOutcome(durable, error)`) returning `{status, failureCode}`, consumed by both.\n\n**A7 — Test monkey-patches a real registry, and the retry swap crosses instances.** `answer-guardless.test.ts:253-254` mutates a constructed `createTerminalLockRegistry()` object; `:393` then assigns `createTerminalLockRegistry().runExclusive` from a *throwaway second registry*. `runExclusive` closes over that registry's private `tails` queue map, so the retry runs against a lock table unrelated to the one the failed attempt touched. Cost: the retry assertion cannot catch a stuck/leaked queue entry left by the failure — the exact regression the test looks like it covers. Right altitude: inject a `locks` dep double for the whole harness rather than patching methods onto a live registry.\n\nNo action needed: `toLedgerFailureCode` in `attempt-ledger.ts:134-139` is correctly placed — narrowing lives with the type it narrows to, and `attempt-ledger.test.ts:54-61` tests it there.\n\nMinor, adjacent: `answer.ts:1362` and `:1364` read `deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)` twice for the same row.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>80149</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>21</tool_uses><duration_ms>231508</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:43:42.590Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a5c6924cf60cb84a8</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_cjt8XP5g7b0MRw1rfZ7cPqVP</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a5c6924cf60cb84a8.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review fix reuse\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>/simplify (reuse angle) on uncommitted diff — 5 modified + 1 new file, all in `packages/host-service/src/companion/`. No edits made. 7 findings, ranked.\n\n**1. Orphaned JSDoc — new export stole `createNotifyingCaptureSink`'s doc block**\n`C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\index.ts:1430-1469`\nThe 15-line comment \"Wraps the store's capture sink with the effects that must fire exactly when custody changes. ORDER MATTERS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS…\" now sits directly above `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish`, which wraps nothing and has no capture sink. `createNotifyingCaptureSink` (1471) is left undocumented in a file where every export carries a doc block.\nCost: the file's densest invariant comment now describes the wrong function; new export ships with zero docs. Fix = move the new function below `createNotifyingCaptureSink` (or above the comment) and give it its own block.\n\n**2. `question.resolved` frame literal now built in 3 places**\n`index.ts:723-731` (`publishSettledQuestion`, outcome `\"unknown\"`), `index.ts:1460-1468` (new), `index.ts:1514-1522` (sink `resolve`, outcome `\"answered\"`). Three copies of the same 9-line `events.publish({t:\"question.resolved\", d:{questionId, resolvedAtMs, resolvedBy, outcome}})`, differing only in provenance source and outcome.\nCost: ~27 lines where ~12 would do; any frame-shape change is a 3-site edit. Fix = `publishQuestionResolved(events, {questionId, resolvedAtMs, resolvedBy, outcome})`.\n\n**3. Publish-containment pattern not reused → 11 lines of try/catch pushed to the caller**\n`answer.ts:1177-1193` vs `index.ts:716-746`.\n`publishSettledQuestion` already establishes the house rule (\"Never throws into the caller: a frame nobody can currently receive must not break…\") by wrapping publish in try/catch + log. `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` does not, so `answer.ts` had to hand-roll its own try/catch + `companion.answer.remote_provenance_failed` log around the dep call.\nCost: 11 added lines in the hot answer path, and containment placed at the call site so any future caller must remember it. Fix = contain inside `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` (semantics identical — the answer.ts catch already swallows everything).\n\n**4. Dep name collides with a still-reachable store method**\n`answer.ts:240` (`questions: QuestionStore`) + `answer.ts:246` (new `markRemoteAnswered(...)`).\n`deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)` still exists on the same deps object with the same name and near-identical signature, but skips the publish. `answer-guardless.test.ts:262` already wires the raw store method into the new slot, so that suite never exercises publishing.\nCost: two same-named APIs one property apart, one silently skipping the corrective frame. Fix = name the dep `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish`, matching the impl (2 call sites, no behaviour change).\n\n**5. Duplicate `LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES` membership test, both via unchecked casts**\n`attempt-ledger.ts:134-138` (new) and `attempt-ledger.ts:549-551` (read boundary).\n`(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly SealedErrorCode[]).includes(code)` vs `(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly string[]).includes(row.failureCode)` — same test, two different casts, plus a third `code as LedgerFailureCode` cast on the result.\nCost: 3 `as` casts in the module whose stated premise is \"the runtime check lives at the read boundary\". Fix = one `isLedgerFailureCode(code: string): code is LedgerFailureCode` predicate; both sites lose all casts (`toLedgerFailureCode` becomes `isLedgerFailureCode(code) ? code : \"internal\"`). Also: `toLedgerFailureCode` is the only undocumented export in that file.\n\n**6. Duplicated pre-write outcome derivation across both catch blocks; one redundant DB read**\n`answer.ts:1328-1345` and `answer.ts:1362-1378`.\n- `deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)` is called twice at 1362 and 1364 for the same row (once for `.status`, once for `.leaseId`) — one extra SQLite read on every error path.\n- `preWrite ? \"failed\" : \"unconfirmed\"` / `preWrite ? \"internal\" : null` appears 4 times across the two blocks (ledger + audit in the first, nested ternary in the second).\nCost: 1 redundant query; status and failureCode are coupled invariants (\"failed ⇒ internal\") re-stated 4×, so they can drift independently. Fix = hoist `const durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)` once, and one `const outcome = preWrite ? {status:\"failed\", failureCode:\"internal\"} as const : {status:\"unconfirmed\", failureCode:null} as const` spread into all sites.\n\n**7. New test re-creates fixtures that already exist**\n`answer-settlement-events.test.ts:13-52` (`source()`, `captureInput()`) duplicates `question-lifecycle.test.ts:26-78` (`resolver()`, `captureInput()`, `questionItem()`) — same `QuestionSourceResolver` shape, same capture-input shape; the existing pair already accepts overrides, the new one doesn't.\nCost: ~40 duplicated fixture lines; a `QuestionSourceResolver` or capture-input field change now breaks 2 suites and must be edited twice. Fix = extract a `companion/test-fixtures.ts` (none exists today) and import from both.\n\nNothing here is a correctness claim — 1, 2, 3, 5, 7 are pure duplication/consistency; 4 and 6 are footgun-shaped but behaviour-neutral as written.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>79987</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>236635</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:44:06.163Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6ff688806d1aec1c</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a6ff688806d1aec1c.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review fix efficiency\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Efficiency review of shared-checkout uncommitted diff (5 modified + 1 new file, all `packages/host-service/src/companion/`).\n\n## Finding 1 — duplicate durable ledger read (real, only material one)\n\n`C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\packages\\host-service\\src\\companion\\answer.ts:1362-1364`\n\n```ts\nconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\nif (durableStatus === \"claimed\" || durableStatus === \"in_flight\") {\n    const durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);\n```\n\nTwo `ledger.get` calls, same `requestId`, same tick, no write between → second is strictly redundant.\n\nConcrete cost: `get` = `selectRow` (`.select()` all columns from `answer_attempts` `WHERE requestId=?` + drizzle row mapping) then `validate` (~10 field/cross-field checks). So one extra SQLite query + row materialization + validation per throw that reaches the outer catch with a `claimed`/`in_flight` row — i.e. every routine SealedError refusal (guard_failed, already_resolved, lease_held), not just rare faults.\n\nFix: read once, derive both.\n\n```ts\nconst durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);\nconst durableStatus = durable?.status;\n```\n\nNote the same path already re-reads at `answer.ts:1328` (inner catch) before `recordOutcome`; that one is not redundant (write intervenes), so the internal-error path legitimately costs 2 reads, 3 today.\n\n## Finding 2 — redundant store lookup + dead branch (low cost, cleanup)\n\n`...\\companion\\index.ts:1445-1467` (`markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish`)\n\n`deps.questions.get(questionId)` is called, then `questions.markRemoteAnswered` does its own `byId.get(questionId)` internally (`question-store.ts:1964-1965`). Both are in-memory `Map` gets → cost negligible (no I/O), so this is style not throughput.\n\nThe `if (question === null) return;` branch is dead as a guard: `markRemoteAnswered` already returns `false` for an unknown id, and the next line returns on `false`. Only `wasResolved` genuinely needs the pre-read. Collapse to reading `state` once:\n\n```ts\nconst wasResolved = deps.questions.get(questionId)?.state === \"resolved\";\nif (!deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)) return;\nif (!wasResolved) return;\n```\n\n## Checked, no issue\n\n- `toLedgerFailureCode` (`attempt-ledger.ts:134-138`): linear `includes` over a 10-element frozen literal, error paths only. Cheaper than a Set/object map at this size — leave it.\n- `answer.ts:1177-1193` try/catch around `markRemoteAnswered`: zero-cost on the non-throwing path.\n- `AnswerDeps.markRemoteAnswered` closure created once per `createAnswerDeps` (`index.ts:2046`), not per request.\n- Test changes (`answer-guardless.test.ts`, `attempt-ledger.test.ts`, new `answer-settlement-events.test.ts`): no sleeps, real timers, or per-test DB spin-up added; new settlement test is pure in-memory. No efficiency debt.\n\nNo edits made.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>62289</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>137525</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:44:06.163Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae444045e6510c1b4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\ae444045e6510c1b4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review fix simplicity\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Reviewed the uncommitted diff in the shared checkout (5 modified + 1 new file, all under `packages/host-service/src/companion/`). Biome clean. Quality findings only, no edits made.\n\n## Findings (ranked)\n\n**1. Doc block detached from its function — `index.ts:1430-1469`**\n`markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` was inserted *between* the docblock (\"Wraps the store's capture sink with the effects that must fire exactly when custody changes… ORDER MATTERS IN BOTH DIRECTIONS… `inner.capture` throws `CaptureRejectedError`…\") and `createNotifyingCaptureSink` at line 1471. That comment now documents the new function; the sink it describes has none.\nCost: IDE hover / any reader of `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` gets the sink's capture-order contract, which is false for it; the sink's real ordering invariant becomes invisible at its definition. Fix: move the block back above line 1471, give the new function its own.\n\n**2. New function's inverted gate is undocumented — `index.ts:1451-1459`**\n```ts\nconst wasResolved = question.state === \"resolved\";\nif (!deps.questions.markRemoteAnswered(...)) return;\nif (!wasResolved) return;   // publish ONLY if it was ALREADY resolved\n```\nReads backwards without context (publish a resolution frame only when it was already resolved). Cost: in a file where comments are the spec, the one branch encoding the PostToolUse-race correction carries zero explanation → next editor \"fixes\" the inversion. Needs one line: the not-yet-resolved case is published by the sink's `resolve`, this path only re-publishes corrected remote provenance.\n\n**3. Duplicated `question.resolved` frame literal — `index.ts:1460-1468` vs `1514-1522`**\nSame 4-field frame constructed in two places. The file already has `publishPendingQuestion` (line 1781, exported and unit-tested) as the extraction precedent.\nCost: renderer wire-contract drift — a field added in one publisher silently missing from the other. Fix: `publishQuestionResolved(deps, { questionId, resolvedAtMs, resolvedBy })`.\n\n**4. Redundant ledger read + dead optional chain — `answer.ts:1362-1377`**\n```ts\nconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\nif (durableStatus === \"claimed\" || durableStatus === \"in_flight\") {\n    const durable = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId);   // second read of the same row\n    ...\n    leaseId: durable?.leaseId ?? null,                    // row provably exists here\n}\n```\nCost: an extra SQLite query on every failing answer, plus a `?? null` fallback that can never fire yet reads as a real case. Fix: one `const durable = deps.ledger.get(...)`, branch on `durable?.status`, then `durable.leaseId`.\n\n**5. Stale comments contradicted by the lines directly under them — `answer.ts:1317-1320` and `1354-1361`**\nBoth paragraphs state the old unconditional rule — \"must not be downgraded to 'never sent'… Unknown outcome, stated as such\" and \"Anything else… degrades to `unconfirmed` rather than to a `failed` that would assert nothing was typed\" — while the new `preWrite` branches (1329-1344, 1365-1374) now record exactly `failed`/`internal` for a non-sealed fault when the row is still `claimed`.\nCost: the spec-comment and the code disagree at the highest-stakes point in the file (whether the wire says bytes may have landed). Highest-value cleanup after #1.\n\n**6. Same mapping spelled 4× / nested ternary — `answer.ts:1330-1345`, `1366-1374`**\n`preWrite ? \"failed\" : \"unconfirmed\"` + `preWrite ? \"internal\" : null` repeated across the `recordOutcome` and `appendAnswerAudit` calls, then re-derived as a 3-level nested ternary in the outer catch.\nCost: four independent edit sites for one rule → attempt row and audit row can diverge. Fix: one `const outcome = preWrite ? { status: \"failed\", failureCode: \"internal\" } as const : { status: \"unconfirmed\", failureCode: null } as const;` spread into both.\n\n**7. Membership check duplicated with two different casts — `attempt-ledger.ts:135` vs `:551`**\nNew `toLedgerFailureCode` does `(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly SealedErrorCode[]).includes(code) ? (code as LedgerFailureCode) : \"internal\"`; line 551 already does `(LEDGER_FAILURE_CODES as readonly string[]).includes(row.failureCode)`. Also the only export in this heavily-documented file with no doc comment.\nCost: three casts where a single `function isLedgerFailureCode(c: string): c is LedgerFailureCode` would serve both sites cast-free at the narrow point.\n\n**8. Error convention split at the same call site — `answer.ts:1177-1193`**\nThe new `markRemoteAnswered` dep is wrapped in `try/catch` + log, while `nudgeRepaint` immediately below returns `{ error }` — two conventions for two adjacent post-write bookkeeping hooks. The dep also types the return as `void` although the underlying store method returns a boolean \"applied\" signal that is discarded at the boundary.\nCost: minor, but the swallowing `try/catch` in `answer.ts` is the kind of masking the repo bans elsewhere; moving it into the adapter lambda (`index.ts:2043-2050`) or matching `nudgeRepaint`'s result shape keeps `answer.ts` free of it.\n\n**9. Test harness borrows a method off a throwaway registry — `answer-guardless.test.ts:253-254`, `394`**\n`locks.runExclusive = options.lockRun` then, mid-test, `harness.deps.locks.runExclusive = createTerminalLockRegistry().runExclusive` — a second full registry allocated purely to steal one closure-bound method (whose own empty lock map is what the retry then uses).\nCost: the restore reads as \"reset the registry\" but is actually \"swap in a different registry's state.\" A `harness.restoreLocks()` helper (or a settable override slot on the harness) says what it does.\n\n**10. New test stubs diverge from the file-local precedent — `answer-settlement-events.test.ts:63-99`**\nThree separate `as never` casts (`push`, two `events`), and two distinct `events` stubs both pushing into the same `frames` array. `question-freshness.test.ts:169-193` already establishes one `as unknown as NotifyingSinkDeps` fixture factory for these deps.\nCost: low — style drift plus a reader wondering whether the two `events` objects are meaningfully different (they aren't).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>75020</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>226166</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
