{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-12T13:51:02.106Z","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0506b2ead9f3cff9</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>call_IbhBaE291g5DGbkVgEMgFgQs</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\083b7de7-951b-4059-a4aa-3dd81cb5a6cf\\tasks\\a0506b2ead9f3cff9.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review simplification opportunities\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Findings below. Caveat up front: another agent (simplify-apply) is actively rewriting these files — mtimes moved twice during analysis and `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` was deleted and re-added mid-read. Line numbers are as of the last read (~13:50); the structural points hold regardless.\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. `packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts:1319-1322` and `:1355-1366` — same pre-write policy encoded twice.**\nBoth catch blocks re-read `deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)` and re-derive \"status `claimed` → `failed`/`internal`, else `unconfirmed`/`null`\". Two ledger reads on one error path, two copies of the rule. Cost: the \"claimed means nothing was typed\" invariant must be edited in two places; a future third failure exit copies it again.\n\n**2. `answer.ts:1360-1366` — 3-branch nested ternary where 2 branches are unreachable.**\n```\nstatus: preWrite || error instanceof SealedError ? \"failed\" : \"unconfirmed\",\nfailureCode: preWrite ? \"internal\" : error instanceof SealedError ? toLedgerFailureCode(error.body.code) : null,\n```\nReaching the outer catch with a live row requires status `claimed` (all pre-lock sealed refusals at `:922/975/988/1004` throw before any outcome is recorded) or `in_flight` (only if the inner catch's own `recordOutcome` throws). `preWrite` therefore wins for every real SealedError, so `toLedgerFailureCode(error.body.code)` — the entire reason the new helper exists — has no reachable production caller. Cost: a new exported function + a new test file section (`attempt-ledger.test.ts:54-61`) guarding dead code, plus the comment directly above it (\":1349-1352\", \"recorded verbatim so §11.4's replay can say why\") now contradicts the code, which records `internal` for everything.\n\n**3. `answer.ts:241-250` — new `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish` dep shadows the existing `questions` dep.**\n`AnswerDeps` already carries `questions: QuestionStore`, whose `markRemoteAnswered` the new callback wraps. Two routes to one store mutation, and every `AnswerDeps` construction site must wire the new one. Cost is already realized: `answer-guardless.test.ts:262` wires `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish: questions.markRemoteAnswered`, i.e. the largest answer suite runs a version of the path with publication silently removed — a seam that exists to be tested is untested there. Passing `events` into `AnswerDeps` (as `index.ts` already does for `AnswerAdapterDeps`) would remove the callback entirely.\n\n**4. `index.ts:1524` — caller re-derives store-internal branch state.**\n`wasResolved = deps.questions.get(questionId)?.state === \"resolved\"` exists only to predict which branch `markRemoteAnswered` takes internally (`question-store.ts:1969`). Two store reads plus a boolean to reconstruct a decision the store already made. Cost: the amend rule now lives in two files; widening the store's amend condition (e.g. to `stale`) desynchronizes the publish decision with no compile error. A richer return (`\"amended\" | \"recorded\" | \"rejected\"`) collapses it to one call.\n\n**5. `index.ts:1432-1437` — `publishQuestionResolved` dedupes 2 of 3 publish sites.**\n`:1497` and `:1534` route through it; `publishSettledQuestion` at `:717-733` still hand-builds the identical frame literal. Partial extraction leaves the drift it was introduced to prevent, and now costs a reader one extra indirection to discover the third site isn't covered.\n\n**6. `index.ts:1504-1508` — `RemoteAnswerPublisherDeps` duplicates `NotifyingSinkDeps`.**\nExact structural subset (`questions`, `events`, `logger`) of the interface 100 lines above it. Cost: two declarations to keep aligned; `Pick&lt;NotifyingSinkDeps, \"questions\" | \"events\" | \"logger\"&gt;` says the same thing and stays correct automatically.\n\n**7. `answer-guardless.test.ts:254` + `:393` — the lock double is installed by two different mechanisms.**\nConstructor option (`lockRun`) sets `locks.runExclusive` by post-hoc mutation at `:254`, then the test mutates it back at `:393` with a throwaway second registry. Cost: the harness option's meaning is \"initial value only\", discoverable only by reading the test body; a reader who assumes the option is authoritative gets it wrong.\n\n**8. `answer-settlement-events.test.ts:13-28, 63-74` — minor.**\n`source()` returns two byte-identical resolvers for `resolveTerminal`/`resolveActiveTerminal`; two `as never` casts stub `push`/`events`. The test's first half (capture → resolve → desktop-provenance assertion) re-covers what `push-retract.test.ts` already sets up, and is only there to reach the resolved state the corrective frame needs. Cost is small, but it forced two `index.ts` internals (`createNotifyingCaptureSink`, `markRemoteAnsweredAndPublish`) to become exports of an already 2,100-line module purely for test reach.\n\n## Out of scope but adjacent (flagging once, not analyzed)\n`answer.ts:1355-1366`: the replay refusal at `:884` (`lease_held`, thrown while the row is legitimately `claimed` by the in-flight original) falls into that outer catch, where `preWrite` is true → it writes `failed`/`internal` over the winner's row. Correctness, not complexity — for `answer-review`/`code-review` to confirm.\n\nSnapshot of the analyzed state is at `C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\\tmp\\simplify-complexity-snap\\` (taken ~13:47, before the last edit wave).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>89737</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>302675</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
