{\n1313\t\t\t\tif (error instanceof SealedError) throw error;\n1314\t\n1315\t\t\t\t// The lock was never taken, so the critical section never ran and nothing\n1316\t\t\t\t// was written. Reported honestly; the client MUST NOT retry a write.\n1317\t\t\t\tif (error instanceof TerminalLockTimeoutError) {\n1318\t\t\t\t\t// LEDGER FIRST, AUDIT SECOND, and the order is load-bearing rather than\n1319\t\t\t\t\t// stylistic. `audit.append` does I/O and can throw; when it went first, a\n1320\t\t\t\t\t// failed audit write skipped the ledger advance entirely and left the\n1321\t\t\t\t\t// claim `in_flight` — \"keystrokes are landing right now\" for a request\n1322\t\t\t\t\t// whose lock was never even taken. `recordOutcome` swallows its own\n1323\t\t\t\t\t// failures, so putting it first cannot cost the audit entry, while the\n1324\t\t\t\t\t// reverse order costs the ledger. The branch below already had this order;\n1325\t\t\t\t\t// this one did not.\n1326\t\t\t\t\tawait recordOutcome(deps, {\n1327\t\t\t\t\t\t...baseAttempt,\n1328\t\t\t\t\t\tstatus: \"failed\",\n1329\t\t\t\t\t\tresolvedAtMs: null,\n1330\t\t\t\t\t\tfailureCode: \"internal\",\n1331\t\t\t\t\t\tguardsPassed: [],\n1332\t\t\t\t\t\tguardsAbstained: [],\n1333\t\t\t\t\t});\n1334\t\t\t\t\tawait appendAnswerAudit(deps, {\n1335\t\t\t\t\t\t...baseAudit,\n1336\t\t\t\t\t\ttsMs: deps.now(),\n1337\t\t\t\t\t\tguards: null,\n1338\t\t\t\t\t\tguardsAbstained: null,\n1339\t\t\t\t\t\toutcome: \"failed\",\n1340\t\t\t\t\t\tfailureCode: \"internal\",\n1341\t\t\t\t\t});\n1342\t\t\t\t\tthrow sealed(\n1343\t\t\t\t\t\t503,\n1344\t\t\t\t\t\t\"internal\",\n1345\t\t\t\t\t\t\"the terminal was busy; nothing was written\",\n1346\t\t\t\t\t);\n1347\t\t\t\t}\n1348\t\t\t\t// (ANSWER-INFLIGHT) Classify from the durable transition, not catch depth.\n1349\t\t\t\t// A still-`claimed` row proves `beginWrite` never ran, so zero bytes landed\n1350\t\t\t\t// and the request may fail without fencing. Once `in_flight`, bytes may have\n1351\t\t\t\t// landed; preserve the duplicate fence and state the outcome as unknown.\n1352\t\t\t\tdeps.log({\n1353\t\t\t\t\tevent: \"companion.answer.internal_error\",\n1354\t\t\t\t\tquestionId: request.questionId,\n1355\t\t\t\t\tterminalId: question.terminalId,\n1356\t\t\t\t\tleaseId: lease.leaseId,\n1357\t\t\t\t\terror: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n1358\t\t\t\t});\n1359\t\t\t\tconst durableStatus = deps.ledger.get(request.requestId)?.status;\n1360\t\t\t\tconst outcome =\n1361\t\t\t\t\tdurableStatus === \"claimed\"\n1362\t\t\t\t\t\t? ({ status: \"failed\", failureCode: \"internal\" } as const)\n1363\t\t\t\t\t\t: ({ status: \"unconfirmed\", failureCode: null } as const);\n1364\t\t\t\tawait recordOutcome(deps, {\n1365\t\t\t\t\t...baseAttempt,\n1366\t\t\t\t\t...outcome,\n1367\t\t\t\t\tresolvedAtMs: null,\n1368\t\t\t\t\tguardsPassed: [],\n1369\t\t\t\t\tguardsAbstained: [],\n1370\t\t\t\t});\n1371\t\t\t\tawait appendAnswerAudit(deps, {\n1372\t\t\t\t\t...baseAudit,\n1373\t\t\t\t\ttsMs: deps.now(),\n1374\t\t\t\t\tguards: null,\n1375\t\t\t\t\tguardsAbstained: null,\n1376\t\t\t\t\toutcome: outcome.status,\n1377\t\t\t\t\tfailureCode: outcome.failureCode,\n1378\t\t\t\t});\n1379\t\t\t\tthrow error;\n1380\t\t\t} finally {\n1381\t\t\t\t// Released on confirmed, on guard_failed, on unconfirmed and on any\n1382\t\t\t\t// unexpected throw. A lease that outlives its answer would lock the\n1383\t\t\t\t// question out until its TTL lapsed.\n1384\t\t\t\tdeps.leases.release(lease.leaseId);\n1385\t\t\t}\n1386\t\t} catch (error) {\n1387\t\t\t// A row still `in_flight` here means the throw got past every path that\n1388\t\t\t// records its own outcome, so this is the last chance to stop it claiming\n1389\t\t\t// forever that keystrokes are landing.\n1390\t\t\t//\n1391\t\t\t// A still-`claimed` row proves the write transition never happened and records\n1392\t\t\t// `failed/internal`. An `in_flight` SealedError is a known refusal, narrowed to\n1393\t\t\t// the ledger's durable code set 