ts\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"a009cd7c-e74e-421b-b1f6-96c52f74891d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"5548f27b-2b1d-4e0e-84da-bd27822c3dc9\",\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"REDACTED\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Fork started — processing in background\"}]},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"<fork-boilerplate>\\nYou are a worker fork. The transcript above is the parent's history — inherited reference, not your situation. You are NOT a continuation of that agent. Execute ONE directive, then stop.\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Do NOT spawn subagents with the Agent tool. The \\\"default to forking\\\" guidance is for the parent; you ARE the fork, execute directly.\\n- One shot: report once and stop. No follow-up questions, no proposed next steps, no waiting for the user.\\n\\nGuidelines (your directive may override any of these):\\n- Stay in scope. Other forks may be handling adjacent work; if you spot something outside your directive, note it in a sentence and move on.\\n- Open with one line restating your task, so the parent can spot scope drift at a glance.\\n- Be concise — as short as the answer allows, no shorter. Plain text, no preamble, no meta-commentary.\\n- If you committed changes, list the paths and commit hashes in your report.\\n</fork-boilerplate>\\n\\nYour directive: You are Phase 1 Simplification reviewer for /simplify. Review ONLY the current uncommitted diff in FEATURES.md, packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts, answer-guardless.test.ts, index.ts. Do not hunt correctness bugs. Flag redundant/derivable state, copy-paste, deep nesting, overlong test scaffolding, dead additions; name simpler equivalent. Return findings with absolute file path, line, one-line summary, concrete maintenance cost. If none, say none. Read current diff/files directly. Do not edit.\"}]},\"uuid\":\"7117e997-08d2-4a25-88c5-0afb24936d6d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-12T17:53:10.166Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"7117e997-08d2-4a25-88c5-0afb24936d6d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"attachment\":{\"type\":\"skill_listing\",\"content\":\"- access-new-relic-logs: Use when you or the user wants to investigate logs for app.\\n- access-outlook-business-email: Use when you or the user wants to read, search, triage, or reason about a Microsoft 365 business mailbox, or draft a reply or new email for the user to review and send. This skill never sends — it only creates drafts.\\n- access-xero-for-px\\n- anvil-yaml\\n- brand-guidelines\\n- call-another-llm: Call Codex CLI or Claude Code CLI with a prompt and save the response to tmp/llm-responses/. Use when you need a second opinion, want to offload a subtask, or compare answers across LLMs.\\n- canvas-design\\n- chatgpt-pro-browser-query\\n- chrome-control: Shared Chrome DevTools control bridge that owns the single CDP connection so multiple Chrome-using skills run from one Chrome \\\"Allow control\\\" approval. Use as STEP 1 of any skill that drives the user's Chrome (ChatGPT browser query, ChatGPT image generation, future browser-driving skills).\\n- chrome-devtools-cli: Use this skill to write shell scripts or run shell commands to automate tasks in the browser or otherwise use Chrome DevTools via CLI.\\n- claude-epic-pr-review\\n- claude-team-review-plan-implementation\\n- client-forms: Create, inspect, edit, style, validate, and convert Anvil client Forms under `client_code`, including Form Python, HTML templates, event wiring, Data Bindings, RepeatingPanel item templates, reusable Forms, theme CSS, and legacy YAML templates.\\n- client-modules\\n- codex-image-generation\\n- data-models\\n- doc-coauthoring\\n- docx\\n- domain-modeling\\n- frontend-design\\n- implement-good-test-patterns\\n- initiate-smoke-tests\\n- mailshot-carehomes\\n- manage-m365-tenant\\n- mk-feature-plan-mode\\n- pdf\\n- performance-metrics-from-new-relic\\n- pptx\\n- pr-swarm-review: Fetch comments from an open GitHub pull request, then use a single sub-agent to extract every reviewer's substantive concerns and validate each one against the codebase in one pass, and consolidate the verdicts back in the main agent for triage and fixing. Use when the user wants existing PR comments triaged before deciding what to fix.\\n- prototype\\n- quick-smoke-tests: Generate an ordered, dependency-aware list of UI smoke tests for the work just completed in this session. Walk the user through every test using the AskUser tool — one question per test with all details embedded in the question — then validate each reported result against the database. Use when the user says \\\"smoke test what we just did\\\", \\\"let's quickly verify the changes in the UI\\\", or similar after implementation work.\\n- server-code: Edit Anvil server code Python modules under `server_code`, including server-callable functions, background logic, and server packages, while keeping changes isolated to the app workspace.\\n- skill-creator\\n- sync-global-user-instructions-md\\n- sync-mcps\\n- taste\\n- theme-factory\\n- webapp-testing\\n- windows-computer-use\\n- xlsx\\n- codex:rescue\\n- codex:setup\\n- feature-dev:feature-dev\\n- superset:superset-automation\\n- astral:ruff\\n- astral:ty\\n- astral:uv\\n- codex:codex-cli-runtime\\n- codex:codex-result-handling\\n- codex:gpt-5-4-prompting\\n- frontend-design:frontend-design\\n- understand-anything:understand\\n- understand-anything:understand-chat\\n- understand-anything:understand-dashboard\\n- understand-anything:understand-diff\\n- understand-anything:understand-domain\\n- understand-anything:understand-explain\\n- understand-anything:understand-knowledge\\n- understand-anything:understand-onboard\\n- superset:10x\\n- superset:automate\\n- superset:contribute\\n- superset:doctor\\n- superset:feedback\\n- superset:orchestrate\\n- superset:setup\\n- superset:standup\\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \\\"chart\\\", \\\"graph\\\", \\\"plot\\\", \\\"data viz\\\", \\\"visualization\\\", \\\"dashboard\\\", \\\"analytics\\\", \\\"visualize data\\\", \\\"categorical colors\\\", \\\"sequential / diverging palette\\\", \\\"stat tile\\\", \\\"sparkline\\\", \\\"heatmap\\\", \\\"legend\\\", \\\"axis\\\", \\\"tooltip\\\", \\\"chart colors\\\", \\\"color by series\\\".\\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\\\"from now on when X\\\", \\\"each time X\\\", \\\"whenever X\\\", \\\"before/after X\\\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\\\"allow X\\\", \\\"add permission\\\", \\\"move permission to\\\"), env vars (\\\"set X=Y\\\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \\\"allow npm commands\\\", \\\"add bq permission to global settings\\\", \\\"move permission to user settings\\\", \\\"set DEBUG=true\\\", \\\"when claude stops show X\\\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \\\"rebind ctrl+s\\\", \\\"add a chord shortcut\\\", \\\"change the submit key\\\", \\\"customize keybindings\\\".\\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \\\"check the deploy every 5 minutes\\\", \\\"keep running /babysit-prs\\\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\\\"run this once at 3pm\\\", \\\"remind me to check X tomorrow\\\").\\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \\\"looks like a one-liner\\\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\\n- init\\n- security-review\",\"skillCount\":79,\"isInitial\":true,\"names\":[\"access-new-relic-logs\",\"access-outlook-business-email\",\"access-xero-for-px\",\"anvil-yaml\",\"brand-guidelines\",\"call-another-llm\",\"canvas-design\",\"chatgpt-pro-browser-query\",\"chrome-control\",\"chrome-devtools-cli\",\"claude-epic-pr-review\",\"claude-team-review-plan-implementation\",\"client-forms\",\"client-modules\",\"codex-image-generation\",\"data-models\",\"doc-coauthoring\",\"docx\",\"domain-modeling\",\"frontend-design\",\"implement-good-test-patterns\",\"initiate-smoke-tests\",\"mailshot-carehomes\",\"manage-m365-tenant\",\"mk-feature-plan-mode\",\"pdf\",\"performance-metrics-from-new-relic\",\"pptx\",\"pr-swarm-review\",\"prototype\",\"quick-smoke-tests\",\"server-code\",\"skill-creator\",\"sync-global-user-instructions-md\",\"sync-mcps\",\"taste\",\"theme-factory\",\"webapp-testing\",\"windows-computer-use\",\"xlsx\",\"codex:rescue\",\"codex:setup\",\"feature-dev:feature-dev\",\"superset:superset-automation\",\"astral:ruff\",\"astral:ty\",\"astral:uv\",\"codex:codex-cli-runtime\",\"codex:codex-result-handling\",\"codex:gpt-5-4-prompting\",\"frontend-design:frontend-design\",\"understand-anything:understand\",\"understand-anything:understand-chat\",\"understand-anything:understand-dashboard\",\"understand-anything:understand-diff\",\"understand-anything:understand-domain\",\"understand-anything:understand-explain\",\"understand-anything:understand-knowledge\",\"understand-anything:understand-onboard\",\"superset:10x\",\"superset:automate\",\"superset:contribute\",\"superset:doctor\",\"superset:feedback\",\"superset:orchestrate\",\"superset:setup\",\"superset:standup\",\"dataviz\",\"update-config\",\"keybindings-help\",\"code-review\",\"simplify\",\"fewer-permission-prompts\",\"loop\",\"schedule\",\"claude-api\",\"run\",\"init\",\"security-review\"]},\"type\":\"attachment\",\"uuid\":\"a79395eb-d100-4151-a808-c25cf9af5f68\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-12T17:53:10.951Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"a79395eb-d100-4151-a808-c25cf9af5f68\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"resp_080e4de16ceed662016a7cb309def08191917b8ab37587e0ce\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"codex-gpt-5-6-sol\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"**Inspecting accidental fork impact**\",\"signature\":\"REDACTED\"}],\"stop_reason\":null},\"attributionAgent\":\"fork\",\"attributionSkill\":\"simplify\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"f265d0ee-77f5-4635-9ce3-d2591d4c3883\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-12T17:53:16.532Z\",\"effort\":\"high\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"f265d0ee-77f5-4635-9ce3-d2591d4c3883\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"resp_080e4de16ceed662016a7cb309def08191917b8ab37587e0ce\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"codex-gpt-5-6-sol\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2782,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":95744,\"output_tokens\":100,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"thinking_tokens\":0},\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"name\":\"PowerShell\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"git diff --unified=40 HEAD -- FEATURES.md packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts\",\"description\":\"Inspect pacing diff for simplification\",\"timeout\":120000}}],\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_details\":null},\"attributionAgent\":\"fork\",\"attributionSkill\":\"simplify\",\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"91aa5f11-7bb0-4b97-a060-25c5e4cc22f8\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-12T17:53:16.788Z\",\"effort\":\"high\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"91aa5f11-7bb0-4b97-a060-25c5e4cc22f8\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"5548f27b-2b1d-4e0e-84da-bd27822c3dc9\",\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"REDACTED\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"<persisted-output>\\nOutput too large (119KB). Full output saved to: C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\.claude\\\\projects\\\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\\\tool-results\\\\b7hcd3cgw.txt\\n\\nPreview (first 2KB):\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'FEATURES.md', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\\nindex b202de796..52e5a4cd7 100644\\n--- a/FEATURES.md\\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\\n@@ -40,81 +40,81 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\\n | Merge semantic review gate | every nightly upstream merge — clean or conflicted — is reviewed by Opus against this manifest BEFORE it may commit/build/publish: a cleanly-merging upstream hunk that semantically breaks a fork feature (the v1.14.2 sidebar-projection incident) is caught here, not in production. Review is fresh after every adaptation and fails closed: no parsable verdict, a final BREAKAGE verdict, or a review run that modified the tree all hard-abort with the baseline untouched | `(MERGE-SEMANTIC-GATE)` |\\n | Nightly proactive port + bounded adaptation | after conflict resolution, Opus compares the old/new upstream tag trees against fork-owned files and surgically ports cleanly-merging API refactors before the gates; semantic BREAKAGE findings then drive at most two repair rounds, each followed by ReferenceError + marker gates and a fresh review. This closes the desktop-v1.16.0 class where upstream removed collections/exports used only by fork files, so git produced no conflict for the resolver to see | `(MERGE-ADAPT)` |\\n...\\n</persisted-output>\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"ff2139b5-42ae-4171-b94e-39ebba9585f3\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-08-12T17:53:22.832Z\",\"toolUseResult\":{\"stdout\":\"warning: in the working copy of 'FEATURES.md', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer-guardless.test.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/answer.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\nwarning: in the working copy of 'packages/host-service/src/companion/index.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it\\ndiff --git a/FEATURES.md b/FEATURES.md\\nindex b202de796..52e5a4cd7 100644\\n--- a/FEATURES.md\\n+++ b/FEATURES.md\\n@@ -40,81 +40,81 @@ in the merge that drops it (the only legitimate way a marker leaves this list).\\n | Merge semantic review gate | every nightly upstream merge — clean or conflicted — is reviewed by Opus against this manifest BEFORE it may commit/build/publish: a cleanly-merging upstream hunk that semantically breaks a fork feature (the v1.14.2 sidebar-projection incident) is caught here, not in production. Review is fresh after every adaptation and fails closed: no parsable verdict, a final BREAKAGE verdict, or a review run that modified the tree all hard-abort with the baseline untouched | `(MERGE-SEMANTIC-GATE)` |\\n | Nightly proactive port + bounded adaptation | after conflict resolution, Opus compares the old/new upstream tag trees against fork-owned files and surgically ports cleanly-merging API refactors before the gates; semantic BREAKAGE findings then drive at most two repair rounds, each followed by ReferenceError + marker gates and a fresh review. This closes the desktop-v1.16.0 class where upstream removed collections/exports used only by fork files, so git produced no conflict for the resolver to see | `(MERGE-ADAPT)` |\\n | Self-repairing build | a failed ARM64 build attempt triggers a bounded Opus 5 repair loop (max 3 attempts / 2 repairs): the repair agent reads the failed job's log, fixes the checked-out tree (scripts/, .github/actions/, source — all take effect the NEXT attempt of the SAME run), and pushes to the repair branch; every deterministic gate re-runs on the repaired sha. Repairs can never change the app version or touch FEATURES.md; workflow-YAML fixes need `WORKFLOW_PUSH_TOKEN` and land next run | `(BUILD-REPAIR)` |\\n | Sidebar hover-freeze | rows never re-sort while the pointer is over the project list (order applies on leave) | `(HOVER-FREEZE)` |\\n | Terminal links | plain click copies a URL/path, Ctrl/Cmd+click opens; `.html` paths open in Chrome (OS default fallback) | `useLinkClickHint`, `openHtmlInBrowser` |\\n | Agent-hook bash-wrap | Gemini/Cursor `.sh` hooks run via Git-for-Windows bash | `agent-wrappers` |\\n | Kanban board | device-local board mirroring branches + Queued column | `v2KanbanCards`, `KANBAN_QUEUE_COLUMN_ID` |\\n | Kanban host-served workspace source | every fork surface that needs the workspace universe (kanban seed/prune/render, bound-card actions, collapse-split, recycle-bin destroy URL, sidebar snooze/archive fallbacks, is-local gate) reads the host-served lists (`useHostWorkspaces`), NEVER the Electric `v2Workspaces` mirror — upstream's offline-first migration stopped writing new rows to the cloud table, so Electric reads silently miss every post-migration branch | `(KANBAN-HOST-SOURCE)` |\\n | Kanban Completed column | fixed FINAL column: dropping a card stamps an editable completed date and hides the thread from the sidebar ENTIRELY (drag out un-completes/restores); per-column date filter (all / last calendar month / custom range) for work-done reports; completed cards survive branch deletion as frozen records; main cards can't complete | `KANBAN_COMPLETED_COLUMN_ID` |\\n | Kanban append-only backup | daily write-once JSON snapshot of the board; code can never delete/overwrite one | `writeKanbanBackup` |\\n | Kanban sidebar button toggles | sidebar Kanban press: anywhere → full-screen board; part-screen split → full-screen (closing then reopens that workspace full size); full-screen → close back to the remembered previous page (fallback Workspaces list) | `(KANBAN-TOGGLE)` |\\n | Subagent tool events never stomp the red | a PostToolUse whose payload carries `agent_id` (ran inside a subagent) maps to the red-respecting SubagentActive — background agents' tool completions must not clear a pending AskUserQuestion/permission red; only a main-loop completion does. SubagentStart likewise | `(SUBTOOL-RED)` |\\n | Async background-tool completions never stomp the red | the Workflow / Agent / Task tools spawn background agents and complete on the MAIN loop (no agent_id) WHILE the main loop is blocked on an AskUserQuestion/permission red — so their PostToolUse maps to SubagentActive, not Start, instead of wrongly clearing the pending red (the SUBTOOL-RED sequential-main-loop assumption breaks for async-spawning tools) | `(ASYNC-TOOL-RED)` |\\n | Untagged background-agent tool completions never stomp the red | teammate/fork tool completions (ordinary Read/Bash/Edit/…) stream in attributed to the PARENT session WITHOUT an agent_id, so the SUBTOOL-RED/ASYNC-TOOL-RED checks miss them and they would Start-clear a pending AskUserQuestion red. A `<terminalId>.askq/` DIR holds one marker PER OWNER (the raising agent_id, or `_main`), set synchronously at PreToolUse:AskUserQuestion; while ANY marker exists an untagged ordinary PostToolUse maps to the red-respecting SubagentActive (and refreshes `.bgactive`, since the blocked main loop means that tool is genuine teammate activity). Per-owner so concurrent questions (main + a subagent) are independent. The renderer has ONE permission axis per terminal, so a single CENTRAL GUARD wraps the whole decision and enforces \\\"rendered red ⇔ `.askq` dir non-empty\\\" in one place: it counts owners before and after the inner decision, then (DOWNGRADE) turns any red-CLEARING result (Start, or a green/blue turn-end) into the red-respecting SubagentActive while any owner remains (stamping `.mainstopped` on a held turn-end so the last SubagentStop still finalizes green), and (UPGRADE) turns a NON-clearing result (SubagentActive yellow/agent/codex hold, or a None no-op) into Start when this event removed the LAST owner — so a just-answered/cancelled/reaped question's red can't latch behind a yellow hold. One choke point, so NO branch — answer, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, SubagentStop (incl. its cancelled-with-question and still-other-work paths), the compact paths — can drop a co-pending red OR strand a resolved one. (The native-notification manager likewise only chimes \\\"complete\\\" on Stop and \\\"awaiting\\\" on permission, never on SubagentActive.) An owner's marker is cleared by its own answer (PostToolUse:AskUserQuestion), its SubagentStop, or the main turn boundary (Stop/UserPromptSubmit clear only `_main`); dead non-`_main` owners are reaped against `background_tasks` ids on Stop/SubagentStop (MARKER-RECONCILE-style) so a subagent that died without a clean stop can't pin red; the whole dir clears on session end / API abort / fresh SessionStart (its hook is registered UNSCOPED so the non-compact source is reached), and a main interrupt clears only `_main` (detached teammates survive). The JSONL watcher emits its direct interrupt `Stop` as `SubagentActive` instead while a live `.askq` owner remains (it bypasses the Python guard). Synchronous + per-owner = no timing window and no cross-question clearing; a tool-PERMISSION red (no `.askq` marker) still clears on its approved tool's main-loop Start — no approval-clear regression. SubagentStop of a subagent that stopped WITH its own question still open emits Start (central guard keeps any other owner) so a cancelled subagent question's red can't latch; the watcher's held interrupt also stamps `.mainstopped` so a later SubagentStop still finalizes green; and an askq owner is NEVER reaped from run-marker age alone (a subagent legitimately BLOCKED on an open question has no activity, so age-reaping it would drop a LIVE red) — when `background_tasks` is absent a leaked owner just errs YELLOW until a session/abort/fresh-start boundary clears it. Accepted limits (all inherent to the fire-and-forget hook→POST→last-writer-wins pipeline, not specific to this feature): a generic Notification (tool-permission) red is unguarded (its approval is indistinguishable from a teammate tool without a request id); and a sub-ms TOCTOU window (a Start in flight before a question opens). Fully closing that TOCTOU window would need pipeline-wide event sequencing | `(UNTAGGED-BG-RED)` |\\n | Working-cadence events unhooked from notify.sh | upstream's raw `notify.sh` passthrough is NOT registered on Claude's UserPromptSubmit / PostToolUse / PostToolUseFailure either (same fix as CLAUDE-STOP-UNHOOKED). The host maps all three to `Start`, and notify.sh bypasses superset-notify.py's central red guard — in a forks/teammates run the forks' PostToolUse completions stream in every 1-5s and each raw `Start` cleared the main loop's pending AskUserQuestion red to yellow. superset-notify.py owns these for Claude (with agent_id discrimination + the guard), so dropping the raw duplicates loses nothing; only binding (SessionStart/SessionEnd) + the native PermissionRequest stay | `(CLAUDE-WORKING-UNHOOKED)` |\\n | Subagent StopFailure stays scoped | a StopFailure carrying an `agent_id` is ONE subagent/fork aborting on a rate-limit/API error, not the whole Claude session dying — it is handled by a SELF-SCOPED inline branch that drops ONLY that fork's run + question markers and never the shared session state (sibling markers, the main `_main` question owner, the agent-bg snapshot), and (like the main-abort branch) never trusts the abort payload's `background_tasks`; the remaining run-marker count + the `.mainstopped` sentinel decide the hold and the central red guard re-holds any still-open question. The full-abort StopFailure branch (clears run_dir + every marker + the `.askq` guard, then greens) would otherwise stomp the main loop's still-pending AskUserQuestion red when a fork hit a limit. Also: superset-notify.py now OWNS `PostToolUseFailure` (aliased to PostToolUse through the red guard) since notify.sh stopped raw-posting it | `(STOPFAIL-SUBAGENT)` |\\n | Layered dot axes | a source's dot status is DERIVED as the highest-precedence active axis (permission > working > review, + the separate blue axes) — events latch/unlatch axes they have evidence about, so a lower assert can never overwrite a higher active state | `applySourceAxes` |\\n | Leaked yellow-hold markers self-heal | a SubagentStop arriving with a mismatched/missing agent_id leaks its run-dir marker and pins the dot yellow with nothing running; at every Stop/SubagentStop the payload's background_tasks[] (ground truth) reaps any marker not listed as still running | `(MARKER-RECONCILE)` |\\n | Subagent markers track per-marker activity | each subagent's run-dir marker is touched on every PostToolUse it fires (not just SubagentStart), so its mtime reflects last activity and the conservative 12h leaked-marker backstop can't false-green a still-working subagent; the COMMON orphan-on-API-error leak is handled fast by the hook's own StopFailure run-dir clear — see `(API-ABORT-RELEASE)` — instead of a flaky short timer | `(MARKER-INACTIVE)` |\\n | Orphaned subagent markers reaped on API abort | a main Claude session's stream abort (`API Error: Stream idle timeout - partial response received`, an isApiErrorMessage line) ends the turn and ORPHANS in-flight subagents WITHOUT firing their SubagentStop, leaking run-dir markers that would pin the dot yellow with nothing running. The abort DOES reach superset-notify.py: Claude Code runs its `StopFailure` hooks whenever the turn's last assistant record is an api-error, so the hook's full-abort StopFailure branch owns the release — it clears the whole run dir plus the sentinel / compact / agent-bg / shell-bg / bg-active markers and the `.askq` guard dir, then greens (a codex-companion job on its OWN API still holds yellow, and the central red guard re-holds any question the abort did not kill). Deterministic, event-driven, no timer, and on the same turn-end path as every other dot decision. The JSONL watcher does NOT own this: an earlier fork revision had it reap off the transcript's isApiErrorMessage line, which duplicated the hook and let a replayed transcript chunk fire a turn-end that stomped the hook's background-blue — see `(WATCHER-BLUE-STOMP)`. The watcher still reads api-errors, but only to signal `(AUTO-RESUME)`; it asserts no dot state for them | `(API-ABORT-RELEASE)` |\\n | Dot state survives renderer reloads | the v2-notifications dot store persists to sessionStorage — an in-place window reload (Ctrl+R / error boundary / crash recovery) no longer wipes every dot; the background-running blue has no self-heal until the next turn end, so a reload used to hide a running background task for hours. Clears on real app restart (no stale dots across launches) | `(DOT-PERSIST)` |\\n | Sidebar agent chips ride the fork dot pipeline | upstream's workspace-agents-row chips (agent logo + status dot under a sidebar row) originally had their own status pipe (host binding raw `lastEventType`, 4-case map that read fork events like SubagentActive/BackgroundRunning as idle) and a broken liveness filter (`terminal_sessions.status` stays `active` forever for a closed-tab pty → chips trapped permission-red for days). Chips now derive status from the shared (AY)/(DOT-AXES) per-source primitive and liveness from the open-pane layout gate — a closed tab's chip is unrepresentable, and a chip can never disagree with the tab/pane/rollup dots. Upstream re-landed a separate facepile chip in desktop-v1.18.0 that never got this fix (its own binding-derived status pipe, so it stale-reds); the fork deletes that chip outright — see `(AGENTS-CHIP-REMOVED)` — leaving the per-tab chips as the only agent chip surface | `(CHIP-DOT-UNIFY)` |\\n | Agent terminals never take OSC shell-blue | the agent CLI (Claude/Codex) is itself the terminal's long-running foreground command, so OSC-133 command-start latches shell-running for the whole agent session; the display primitive applies shell-running blue only to terminals with NO agent axis data — an idle agent tab shows green, and blue on agent terminals comes solely from the background-tasks axis | `(AGENT-SHELL-BLUE)` |\\n | Sidebar per-tab chips | a sidebar workspace row with 2+ open tabs replaces its inline name-line dots with one chip PER TAB — always expanded (no hover-collapse), in exact `paneLayout.tabs[]` order, labelled with the real tab title (override → pane override → per-kind default → `Tab N`; `max-w-28` truncate + full-title tooltip), agent icon from the tab's terminal-pane binding (active pane first, generic terminal glyph for plain-shell tabs), status dot as a corner badge on the icon folded from the tab's pane sources via the shared display-status primitive, flex-wrap rows. ≤1 tab keeps the upstream look: a single folded name dot, no chips. Chip click deep-links to the workspace and focuses that tab (`terminalId`/`chatSessionId`, or a validated `tabId` param for tabs with no domain-id pane, guarded on pane-layout readiness + tab existence). With the workspace-agents-row experiment off, the row falls back to a single name dot folded across ALL tabs (never zero status surfaces) | `(TAB-CHIPS)` |\\n | Upstream agents facepile chip removed | upstream desktop-v1.18.0 added a second agent surface to the sidebar — an \\\"agents\\\" chip (red bot icon + running-agent count, hover card with Stop / Stop-all) rendered by `DashboardSidebarWorkspaceChips` directly beneath the fork's per-tab chips row. It duplicated information (TAB-CHIPS) already shows per tab, and its status feed was an unfixed pre-(CHIP-DOT-UNIFY) copy — binding-derived, no open-pane liveness gate — so it trapped a stale red. The fork DELETES it: the chip, its `DashboardSidebarAgentAvatar` / `DashboardSidebarAgentHoverRow` children, and the now-orphaned `useDashboardSidebarWorkspaceRunningAgents` / `useDashboardSidebarAgentKill` hooks are gone, and the chips strip is ports-only. Its Stop-agent / Stop-all capability is intentionally NOT ported anywhere. The `workspace-agents-row` flag/store stays — it also gates the (TAB-CHIPS) row. A merge that re-imports `DashboardSidebarAgentsChip` must delete it again | `(AGENTS-CHIP-REMOVED)` |\\n | Nightly self-repairs ReferenceError resolutions | the nightly merge job runs the build's cannot-find-name gate itself (pure-JS `--ignore-scripts` install, linux-safe) right after conflict resolution; on failure Opus gets the exact diagnostics and repairs ONLY those (restore dropped imports/declarations), 2 attempts, re-gated each time, still-dirty aborts as before. Converts the import-dropping-resolution class (desktop-v1.15.1 useChangesTab incident) from red-night+manual-recovery to self-healing; the build's own gate stays as defense in depth | `(REFERR-REPAIR)` |\\n | Agent hook fork-diet | every agent lifecycle hook parses + escapes JSON with bash builtins (read, `[[ =~ ]]`, parameter expansion); the old grep/tr + sed pipelines forked ~30 subprocesses per call and crashed the x64-emulated msys2 runtime on Windows ARM64 (the add_item errno-1 fork cascade that wedged every chat's hooks). Wire payload unchanged | `(HOOK-FORK-DIET)` |\\n | Master cards archive-only | a repo's master / non-git master card (`type === \\\"main\\\"`) can never be hard-removed from the sidebar — \\\"Remove from Sidebar\\\" archives it (recoverable under the project's Archived section) instead of hiding it into oblivion. `archiveWorkspace` inserts an archived row for an auto-included main that has none yet. The missing-worktree error page offers Archive (not Delete) for mains; the delete/complete guards resolve the workspace type host-record-first and FAIL LOUD (refuse) when the type is unresolvable instead of failing open on a cold cache; Empty Recycle Bin defensively skips mains | `(MASTER-ARCHIVE-ONLY)` |\\n | Recycle Bin (soft delete) | the default \\\"Delete\\\" for a worktree/branch workspace (sidebar + kanban, incl. unbound Queued cards) is now a lossless, recoverable move-to-bin (silent — no dialog/toast; worktree + branch + terminals untouched, exactly like Archive). Restore always returns to ACTIVE; nothing is ever auto-purged. Permanent git destroy is relocated to \\\"Delete permanently\\\" inside the bin (the old destroy dialog) + a one-confirm \\\"Empty Recycle Bin\\\". A renderer-only retention setting (default 30 days) is a DISPLAY filter only — the bin shows items deleted within the last N days, older ones collapse behind a per-bin \\\"Show all\\\" toggle with an \\\"N hidden by filter\\\" footer. Per-project sidebar \\\"Recycle Bin (N)\\\" section + per-column kanban sub-section, sorted by `deletedAt` DESC. Mains stay archive-only (never deletable) | `(RECYCLE-BIN)` |\\n | Auto-resume stuck chats | when a Claude chat dies on an API failure the JSONL watcher classifies it (deny-list-first: rate-limit-with-reset-time / transient-rate-limit / server-error / connection-drop / half-stop are resumable; auth/policy/bad-image/model-unavailable are notify-only) after a finality/quiescence gate, and a durable MAIN-side registry auto-sends \\\"resume from exactly where everything was left\\\" — fire-at-parsed-reset-time (+30s, DST-correct, fire-now-if-already-past, ≤8d sane cap) for usage limits, else a 60s→×3 backoff capped at 5 sends / 24h. Every send is gated at fire time (toggle on, still the last meaningful transcript line, host-service `commandRunning`=idle + agent-session binding match) so it can never auto-confirm a menu, land mid-turn, or run at a bare shell. Any trusted interaction with that terminal cancels (persisted tombstone survives reload/restart). Account-wide cohorts are jittered + concurrency-capped. Global toggle (default on). Claude-only in v1 — Codex auto-resume is deferred (the on-disk corpus has no validated standalone usage-limit signal). Dots untouched (separate IPC variant + toasts) | `(AUTO-RESUME)`, `autoResumeManager`, `writeInputIfIdleSession`, `useAutoResumeActivity`, `AutoResumeController` |\\n | Dot-decision log + codex pid-reuse cap | superset-notify.py writes an always-on bounded `~/.superset/logs/dot-decisions.log` line at each terminal turn-end decision (the POSTed eventType + WHY a hold won — codex job / background_tasks / subagent markers / GREEN / BLUE / RED), and `_codex_job_active` age-caps pid-bearing `running` codex job records at 6h so a recycled PID can't pin the dot yellow forever (a cap-skipped stale record is logged, so a premature-green is auditable) | `_decision_log` |\\n | Idle/zombie teammate hold reaped | an agent set Claude Code still reports `running` in background_tasks[] but that has produced NO activity (SubagentStart / subagent-scoped PostToolUse refresh a `.bgactive` timer) for 15 min stops pinning the lead's dot yellow — the harness never flips an idle long-lived teammate (or one that died without a clean SubagentStop) to a finished status, so the hold previously lasted until the team was disbanded / the session ended. The JSONL watcher re-asserts yellow if a reaped set is in fact still writing. A stale reap of a MIXED set no longer greens live work: `_split_background` tracks live `type:\\\"shell\\\"` entries separately, so zombie agents + running background shells resolve to the blue shell remainder (live 2026-07-21 incident: two running crash/build monitors showed no dot after the zombie-teammate hold staled out); an agent-only zombie set still greens | `(BG-STALE)` |\\n | Idle teammates green deterministically | Claude Code reports finished teammates in the Stop payload's background_tasks[] as `status:\\\"running\\\"` FOREVER (captured live: 33 teammate entries, all \\\"running\\\", for a session whose 44 teammates had all finished hours earlier) — and the (BG-STALE) reap only runs inside hook events, so a session whose LAST event landed while the set was <15 min idle latched yellow indefinitely (nothing ever re-evaluated; 9 terminals observed stuck). superset-notify.py now keeps a per-terminal teammate LEDGER parsed incrementally from the lead session transcript (payload `transcript_path`; offset+state cached in `<terminalId>.teamstate.json`, keyed by transcript path so /resume self-invalidates): Agent-spawn / SendMessage-wake / non-idle teammate- or agent-messages mark a name active, an `idle_notification` teammate-message marks it idle. At Stop/SubagentStop, when EVERY tracked teammate is provably idle, unfinished teammate-type entries are dropped BEFORE the split/snapshot/hold decision — the lead greens (or blues on a shell remainder) at its actual final turn end instead of holding forever. Positive proof only: no transcript / no teammates seen / any parse error / any teammate not idle → entries kept (old behaviour + (BG-STALE) unchanged behind it); raw fork agentIds (\\\"a\\\"+hex) are excluded from the predicate (forks have real hook lifecycles and would never idle-notify) — and NAMED forks are excluded too: the spawn parser reads the Agent tool-use input's `subagent_type`, skips tracking fork spawns regardless of name, and indexes them by tool-use-id so their completed task-notification untracks exactly that spawn (a named fork previously latched \\\"active\\\" forever and made the all-idle predicate unsatisfiable — live 2026-07-21); cache schema v2 forces one full rescan to self-heal already-poisoned ledgers; subagent/workflow/shell entries always pass through untouched; bg_ids marker reconciliation still uses the RAW list. Decision log carries a `[teammate-idle: dropped N ...]` suffix for auditability. (TEAM-ENTRY-MATCH, 2026-07-22): the all-idle predicate proved unsatisfiable in real multi-agent sessions — 55 permanently-\\\"active\\\" ledger names (named non-fork Agent spawns like sol/general-purpose/review agents that finish via task-notification and never idle-notify, forks re-marked active by a later SendMessage, and \\\"name [hash]\\\" duplicate-suffix splits) held 25 finished wrangler teammates yellow all day. The decision is now PER ENTRY: each running teammate entry's `description` (spawn-prompt head, first ~50 chars + \\\"...\\\") is matched against normalized prompt prefixes recorded at every named Agent spawn (cache schema v3 adds `prompts`; v2 caches force one self-healing full rescan); an entry drops only when it matches ≥1 recorded spawn AND every matching name is provably idle — unmatched/ambiguous-with-active/too-short descriptions keep their entry. SendMessage to a known fork name no longer marks it active, and \\\" [hash]\\\"-suffixed teammate-message ids mirror onto the base name so the base tracks the latest same-named instance. (HOOK-STDIN-UTF8, 2026-07-29): the hook read its payload from TEXT-MODE stdin, which on Windows decodes with the locale code page (cp1252) - any non-ASCII payload character mojibaked, so a teammate entry whose description carried an em-dash could NEVER prefix-match its correctly-decoded transcript prompt and held the lead terminal yellow forever with zero agents running (captured live: care-vision terminal, entry t1ryk141d). Payloads are now byte-read and UTF-8-decoded in both embedded hook scripts. (TEAM-KEPT-DEBUG) logs every teammate entry's per-decision match verdict (normalized prefix, matching names + ledger states, raw entry shape) to ~/.superset/logs/teammate-idle-debug.log (2MB rotate), and the decision-log teammate-idle note now also appears when entries are kept with none dropped | `(TEAMMATE-IDLE)` `(TEAM-ENTRY-MATCH)` `(HOOK-STDIN-UTF8)` `(TEAM-KEPT-DEBUG)` |\\n | ReferenceError-class build gate | the build (and therefore every nightly, whose build job calls the same workflow) fails on cannot-find-name tsc diagnostics ONLY (TS2304/2552/2662/2663/18004) — bare identifiers esbuild bundles fine but that throw ReferenceError at runtime, exactly what a CLEAN-merging upstream hunk can leave in fork code the AI resolver never sees (v1.14.0 layout.tsx incident: upstream renamed the workspace source variable in a non-conflicting hunk; fork's cache-first hold kept referencing it; every v2 workspace view crashed). Per-package direct tsc (turbo graph is debt-blocked); the accepted type debt has zero of these codes so the gate can't false-abort | `(REFERR-GATE)` |\\n | Nightly dependency-consistency + lock regen | the nightly merge can introduce dependency-state drift that only the build discovers (after a windows-arm64 runner spins up): a stale fork root `overrides` entry that pins a package to a version different from the merged workspace pin (electron-builder \\\"production dependency not found …\\\"), or a `bun.lock` left stale by an upstream package.json bump (frozen-install fails). The merge now runs an override-consistency gate (fails loud on any exact override↔pin mismatch) + regenerates `bun.lock` to match the merged manifests with the build's exact bun version (which also fails loud, in the cheap merge job, if the merged manifests don't resolve or a pin is age-blocked) — so this class of build failure is caught/fixed before the windows-arm64 build spins up, baseline untouched on failure (recovery loop). No full `--frozen-lockfile` install runs here: the merge runner is linux, so a platform-specific install quirk would be a false abort, and the regenerated lock is consistent with the manifests by construction. The fork carries NO mastra `overrides` and pins `@mastra/core`/`mastracode` to upstream's exact versions in all three workspaces (upstream keeps mastra consistent via identical exact pins; the seed commit's redundant override + a recovery merge that resolved the pins DOWN to 0.18.0 were the recurring breakage, both corrected) | `(LOCK-REGEN)` |\\n | Fork-owned workflow dir | `.github/workflows/` carries ONLY the fork's own workflows — upstream CI files were dropped at fork creation (they target upstream's infra/secrets) and the Actions `GITHUB_TOKEN` can NEVER push a workflow-file change anyway (no `workflows` scope exists for it: \\\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow\\\"). Upstream desktop-v1.14.3 added `deploy-relay.yml`, which merged CLEANLY and then killed the candidate push after every gate had already passed. The nightly now deterministically restores the fork's exact workflow set mid-merge (`git checkout --no-overlay HEAD -- .github/workflows`) BEFORE conflict detection, so upstream workflow additions/edits never enter the fork, never reach the AI resolver, and never hit the push restriction — and the dropped upstream CI delta is SURFACED, not silently erased (job summary + log + handed to the semantic review, which appends advisory `CI-NOTE:` lines when upstream moves build-relevant logic into its CI), with a pre-commit postcondition proving the committed tree carries zero workflow-dir changes. Wanting an upstream workflow = deliberate local commit with a user token | `(WORKFLOW-FORK-OWNED)` |\\n | Nightly pipeline integrity | three cheap deterministic guards on the merge→build→advance hand-offs: a fatal (non-conflict) `git merge` failure is caught via missing MERGE_HEAD instead of masquerading as a clean merge (the `set +e` wrapper accepts every nonzero exit); the build + advance jobs consume the merge commit's immutable SHA — not the mutable `nightly/*` branch name — under a workflow-level `concurrency` group (queue, never cancel), and advance refuses to fast-forward main if the candidate ref moved; `gh release create` tags the exact built commit via `--target` + a post-publish remote-tag assert (without `--target` gh tags the DEFAULT branch tip, which during a nightly is pre-advance main — every nightly-published release tag pointed one commit behind the installer's true source until 2026-07-11) | `(NIGHTLY-INTEGRITY)` |\\n | Companion keep-awake | the phone/watch companion is worthless if the desktop sleeps mid-question: the answer path lives in host-service, which dies with the machine. The main process ho\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"persistedOutputPath\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\.claude\\\\projects\\\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\\\tool-results\\\\b7hcd3cgw.txt\",\"persistedOutputSize\":121809},\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"91aa5f11-7bb0-4b97-a060-25c5e4cc22f8\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\khair\\\\Code Projects\\\\fork-superset-windows-arm64\",\"sessionId\":\"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\",\"version\":\"2.1.228\",\"gitBranch\":\"main\",\"slug\":\"jolly-singing-lemur\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"ff2139b5-42ae-4171-b94e-39ebba9585f3\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"ab9207100517be12f\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"resp_