{"parentUuid":"451ac499-0a43-4ceb-89e2-c076fdb36e51","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"simplify","path":"bundled:simplify","content":"Review target: `Review and simplify the current uncommitted race-fix diff for claimed zero-write classification and settlement provenance event correction. Complete all phases and apply safe findings.`\n\n`/simplify → 4 cleanup agents in parallel → apply the fixes`\n\nYou are improving the quality of the changed code, not hunting for bugs. Review\nit for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude issues, then fix what you\nfind. Do not look for correctness bugs — that is what `/code-review` is for.\n\n## Phase 0 — Gather the diff\n\nRun `git diff @{upstream}...HEAD` (or `git diff main...HEAD` / `git diff HEAD~1`\nif there's no upstream) to get the unified diff under review. If there are\nuncommitted changes, or the range diff is empty, also run `git diff HEAD` and\ninclude the working-tree changes in scope — the review often runs before the\ncommit. If a PR number, branch name, or file path was passed as an argument,\nreview that target instead. Treat this diff as the review scope.\n\n## Phase 1 — Review (4 cleanup agents in parallel)\n\nLaunch **4 independent review agents** via the Agent tool, all in a\nsingle message so they run concurrently. Pass each agent the diff and one of\nthe four angles below. Each returns its findings with `file`, `line`, a\none-line `summary`, and the concrete cost (what is duplicated, wasted, or\nharder to maintain).\n\n### Reuse\n\nFlag new code that re-implements something the codebase\nalready has — Grep shared/utility modules and files adjacent to the change,\nand name the existing helper to call instead.\n\n### Simplification\n\nFlag unnecessary complexity the diff adds: redundant or derivable state,\ncopy-paste with slight variation, deep nesting, dead code left behind. Name\nthe simpler form that does the same job.\n\n### Efficiency\n\nFlag wasted work the diff introduces: redundant computation or repeated I/O,\nindependent operations run sequentially, blocking work added to startup or\nhot paths. Also flag long-lived objects built from closures or captured\nenvironments — they keep the entire enclosing scope alive for the object's\nlifetime (a memory leak when that scope holds large values); prefer a\nclass/struct that copies only the fields it needs. Name the cheaper\nalternative.\n\n### Altitude\n\nCheck that each change is implemented at the right depth, not as a fragile\nbandaid. Special cases layered on shared infrastructure are a sign the fix\nisn't deep enough — prefer generalizing the underlying mechanism over adding\nspecial cases.\n\n## Phase 2 — Apply the fixes\n\nWait for all four agents to complete, dedup findings that point at the same\nline or mechanism, and fix each remaining one directly. Skip any finding whose\nfix would change intended behavior, require changes well outside the reviewed\ndiff, or that you judge to be a false positive — note the skip rather than\narguing with it. Finish with a brief summary of what was fixed and what was\nskipped (or confirm the code was already clean).\n"},{"name":"windows-computer-use","path":"userSettings:windows-computer-use","content":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\skills\\windows-computer-use\n\n<purpose>\r\nGive an agent full, faithful control of the local Windows desktop — anything a\r\nuser can do with mouse, keyboard, and screen — through one deterministic CLI.\r\nUI Automation (UIA) is primary: enumerate windows, read element trees as text,\r\nand target controls by AutomationId / Name / ControlType. Screenshots + absolute\r\ncoordinate input are the vision fallback for surfaces UIA cannot see.\r\n</purpose>\r\n\r\n<constraints>\r\n- WINDOWS ONLY. Must run in the user's interactive session (not a headless service). uv auto-installs deps from the script's PEP-723 header.\r\n- UIA-FIRST, VISION FALLBACK. Resolve targets by AutomationId → Name+ControlType → path. There is NO automatic CLI fallback: when the CLI returns `target_not_found` on a canvas / custom-drawn surface (or some Electron / games), the ORCHESTRATOR explicitly switches to a screenshot + `--at x,y` coordinate click. The CLI never silently downgrades from UIA to pixels.\r\n- COORDINATES ARE VIRTUAL-DESKTOP SPACE. Primary monitor top-left = (0,0); monitors left/above are negative. Mouse input uses SendInput ABSOLUTE|VIRTUALDESK so clicks land correctly across mixed-DPI monitors. The process is Per-Monitor-V2 DPI aware.\r\n- INPUT IS A GLOBAL SERIAL RESOURCE. One mouse, one keyboard, one foreground window. Exactly ONE action at a time across the whole machine. Read-only inspection (tree/find/screenshot/windows/monitors) is safe to run concurrently.\r\n- FAIL FAST AND LOUD. Every command prints one JSON object: success → stdout `{\"ok\":true,...}`; failure → stderr `{\"ok\":false,\"error\":...,\"reason\":...}` + non-zero exit. Never guess past ambiguity.\r\n- BROWSER WORK → use chrome-control / browser skills, not this skill. Only drive a browser here as a last resort when those are unavailable.\r\n</constraints>\r\n\r\n<orchestration>\r\nRun this skill in a fork-orchestrated loop so heavy output (UIA trees, screenshots) never lands in the main context.\r\n\r\n- MAIN (orchestrator): holds the objective, a rolling sub-goal plan, and a running model of UI state built from fork reports. NEVER calls win_use.py directly; never holds a tree or screenshot. Decomposes the objective into coherent sub-goals, dispatches them, reads compact reports, re-plans from what actually happened.\r\n- ACTION FORK (exactly one, persistent): owns the perceive→act loop for one sub-goal at a time. Main keeps feeding it successive sub-goals. All trees/screenshots stay in its context; it returns only a compact report. RECYCLE it when switching to a different app/window (clean re-orientation boundary) or after a long run of sub-goals.\r\n- PERCEPTION FORKS (many, parallel, read-only): inspect background windows, verify a result, or describe the screen. Safe to fan out because they never touch mouse/keyboard/focus.\r\n\r\nSpawn forks with the Agent tool (`subagent_type: \"fork\"`) so they inherit main's context + prompt cache; address the persistent action fork via SendMessage.\r\n</orchestration>\r\n\r\n<fork_contract>\r\nThe action fork is a FAITHFUL EXECUTOR, not a problem-solver.\r\n\r\n- Execute the instructed sub-goal exactly. Handle normal UI flow on its own: dismiss unexpected non-destructive dialogs, retry an action that had no visible effect, proceed through auth/login prompts.\r\n- STOP AND REPORT (do not improvise a workaround) on either trigger:\r\n  1. TARGET NOT FOUND / AMBIGUOUS — the instructed element is missing, or multiple match and it cannot disambiguate (CLI returns reason `target_not_found` or `ambiguous`).\r\n  2. UNEXPECTED IRREVERSIBLE ACTION — a Delete / Send / Overwrite / Discard / Empty / Format / Uninstall / Pay confirmation that is NOT listed in the sub-goal's `sanctioned_irreversible`. The FORK (the LLM) detects this itself by inspecting the UIA tree BEFORE clicking a Delete/Send/etc. button — read the dialog/button text from the tree and decide. The CLI does NOT enforce this; it only provides the inspect + click primitives. Irreversible actions the sub-goal explicitly sanctioned are carried out.\r\n- Log every action (the CLI writes the action log automatically).\r\n\r\nON A FORK STOP: main spawns a read-only perception fork to diagnose the real screen state, then main decides — correct the sub-goal and re-dispatch (find another solution), OR escalate to the user via AskUser with a cropped screenshot (SendUserFile). Forks never improvise; main may.\r\n</fork_contract>\r\n\r\n<workflow>\r\n1. Main: read the objective; decompose into the next coherent sub-goal with its `sanctioned_irreversible` list.\r\n2. Action fork: orient — `windows` then `tree`/`find` on the target window (text, cheap). Use a screenshot only when UIA is blind.\r\n3. Action fork: act via the smallest reliable primitive (UIA invoke or real SendInput click/type/key), verifying the resulting state from the UIA tree, not pixels.\r\n4. Action fork: on success return a compact report; on a stop trigger return the blocker + small evidence (cropped element screenshot or trimmed tree slice).\r\n5. Main: update its UI-state model, decide the next sub-goal or escalate, recycle the action fork on app/window switch.\r\n</workflow>\r\n\r\n<scripts>\r\nAll commands (quote the path — repo path may contain spaces):\r\n  uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" <command> [options]\r\n\r\nGlobal options: `--log <path>` action log (JSONL, default `WIN_USE_LOG` env or `./tmp/win_use_actions.jsonl`); `--daemon` / `WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` route this call to a running daemon; `--host` / `--port` / `--port-file` locate it.\r\n\r\nRead-only (parallel-safe):\r\n- `monitors` — monitors (rect, size, work area, dpi, scale, primary) + virtual-screen bounds + dpi mode.\r\n- `windows [--all]` — top-level windows: hwnd, title, class, pid, rect, visible, minimized, monitor, z-order.\r\n- `tree --window <hwnd|title> [--depth 14] [--max-nodes 4000]` — UIA element tree (control_type, name, automation_id, class, rect, enabled/offscreen when notable).\r\n- `find --window <w> [--name S] [--control T] [--automation-id ID] [--limit 50]` — matching elements with rect + `path`.\r\n- `screenshot --out <path> [--monitor N | --window <w> | --region x,y,w,h]` — PNG. Default = whole virtual desktop. `--monitor` index is from `monitors`.\r\n\r\nActions (serial — one at a time):\r\n- `focus --window <w>` — restore if minimized + bring to foreground.\r\n- `click [--window <w> (--name|--control|--automation-id) [--index N] | --at x,y] [--button left|right|middle] [--double] [--method input|invoke]` — `input` = real SendInput mouse to element center (default, most faithful); `invoke` = UIA InvokePattern (windowless).\r\n- `type --text \"<t>\" [--window <w> [(--name|--control|--automation-id) [--index N]]] [--method keys|setvalue]` — with a target it clicks to focus then types; `setvalue` uses UIA ValuePattern. Literal text is auto-escaped.\r\n- `key (--combo \"ctrl+shift+s\" | --raw \"<send_keys>\") [--window <w>]` — combos accept ctrl/alt/shift/win + named keys (enter, esc, tab, f1–f24, up…) ; `--raw` passes pywinauto send_keys syntax.\r\n- `scroll [--window <w> (--name|--control|--automation-id) | --at x,y] --amount N [--horizontal]` — wheel notches (+up / −down).\r\n- `drag --start x,y --end x,y [--button left|right|middle]` — press-move-release in virtual-desktop coords.\r\n- `wait --window <w> [--name|--control|--automation-id] [--timeout 10] [--poll 0.3]` — poll until an element appears.\r\n\r\nDaemon (optional — keeps UIA/COM warm, ~halves per-call latency by skipping init):\r\n- `[--port N] daemon` — start the daemon (blocks; run it in the background). Default `--port 0` = OS-assigned; writes a port-file (`./tmp/win_use_daemon.json`) with the actual port + pid and warms UIA on start. `--host` is LOOPBACK-ONLY (`127.0.0.1` / `localhost` / `::1`); any other host fails loud (`reason: unsafe_host`). A second daemon on the same port-file refuses to start (`reason: daemon_already_running`).\r\n- `daemon --status` / `daemon --stop` — check or cleanly stop a running daemon. UIA/command work runs on ONE dedicated worker thread while the accept loop answers control messages itself, so `--status` / `--stop` stay responsive even while a long command (e.g. `wait`) is in flight.\r\n- Route any command through it: `--daemon <command …>` (or set `WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` once in a fork so every call routes; locate the daemon with `WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT` or `--port` / `--port-file`). The daemon serialises all UIA/command work on its one COM-safe worker thread — which also enforces one-action-at-a-time. If routing is requested but no daemon is reachable, the call fails loud (`reason: no_daemon` / `daemon_unreachable`).\r\n- Pass ABSOLUTE paths for `--out` when routed: the daemon resolves relative paths against its OWN working directory (the orchestrator absolutizes `--out` against the client cwd before forwarding). `--log` is forwarded automatically.\r\n- WINDOWS env vars: set them in PowerShell with `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` / `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT=53999` (the inline `VAR=val cmd` form is bash-only).\r\n</scripts>\r\n\r\n<inputs>\r\nSub-goal (main → action fork), conceptual shape:\r\n- `objective`: one coherent task ending at a stable, verifiable UI state (e.g. \"fill and submit the login form in window 'X'\").\r\n- `target_hints`: window title/hwnd and any known AutomationId / Name to aim at.\r\n- `sanctioned_irreversible`: an orchestrator → fork sub-goal field (NOT a CLI flag) — the explicit list of irreversible confirmations this sub-goal may push through (e.g. `[\"delete file report.csv\"]`). The fork (LLM) checks each Delete/Send/Overwrite/etc. dialog it sees in the UIA tree against this list; anything irreversible NOT listed → stop. The CLI is unaware of it.\r\n- `success_criteria`: the observable UIA state that means done.\r\n</inputs>\r\n\r\n<outputs>\r\nReport (action fork → main), compact:\r\n- `outcome`: success | partial | failed | stopped.\r\n- `actions`: resolved targets acted on (by Name/AutomationId, not raw pixels).\r\n- `final_state`: the 2–3 UIA facts main cares about (not the whole tree).\r\n- on stop: `reason` — either a CLI reason (`target_not_found` | `ambiguous`) or a fork-raised `unexpected_irreversible` (the LLM's own decision from reading the dialog text in the UIA tree; not a CLI code) — plus small evidence (cropped element screenshot path or trimmed tree slice).\r\n\r\nAction log: append-only JSONL at `--log` path; one record per action with timestamp, resolved target, method, and result. The audit trail — pass a consistent `--log` across a session.\r\n</outputs>\r\n\r\n<validation>\r\n- Before acting, resolve the target; a single unambiguous element must match, else the CLI returns `ambiguous` (with all candidates) or `target_not_found` — both are stop triggers, exit 1.\r\n- Verify outcomes from the UIA tree / element state, not from the screenshot, whenever a structured value exists (e.g. a display/value/toggle state).\r\n- Treat any non-zero exit / `{\"ok\":false}` as a hard signal; read `reason` and report up rather than retrying blindly.\r\n- Disambiguate `ambiguous` with `--index N` (or a more specific Name/AutomationId) only when main/the plan knows which one is correct.\r\n- CLI `reason` codes you may see: `target_not_found`, `ambiguous`, `index_out_of_range`, `stale_target` (UI changed mid-resolution), `invoke_failed`, `setvalue_failed`, `setvalue_needs_target` (setvalue with no element target), `focus_denied` (could not foreground the window), `window_not_found`, `window_ambiguous` (title matched >1 window), `window_minimized` (screenshot a restored window first), `wait_timeout`, `usage_error` (bad CLI args), `unsupported_platform` (not Windows), and daemon codes `no_daemon`, `daemon_unreachable`, `daemon_protocol`, `daemon_already_running`, `unsafe_host`, `message_too_large`.\r\n</validation>\r\n\r\n<limits>\r\nHard OS walls (not bugs — report and escalate, don't fight):\r\n- UAC secure desktop (elevation prompts) and the lock screen are off-limits — UIA and SendInput cannot cross them.\r\n- A non-elevated process cannot send input to elevated (admin) windows (UIPI). To drive elevated apps, run win_use.py elevated.\r\n- Games / DirectX exclusive-fullscreen expose no UIA and may capture black via GDI — best-effort only.\r\n- UIA on Electron/Qt/Java apps can be shallow or absent; fall back to screenshot + `--at`.\r\n</limits>\r\n\r\n<examples>\r\n- Map the screen:    uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" monitors\r\n- List windows:      uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" windows\r\n- Read a window:     uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" tree --window \"Settings\"\r\n- Find a control:    uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" find --window \"Settings\" --control Button --name \"Bluetooth\"\r\n- Click it:          uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" click --window \"Settings\" --automation-id \"BluetoothToggle\"\r\n- Type into a field: uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" type --window \"Run\" --text \"notepad\"\r\n- Key chord:         uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" key --combo \"ctrl+s\"\r\n- Screenshot screen: uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" screenshot --monitor 0 --out tmp/shots/screen.png\r\n- Vision fallback:   uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" click --at 1493,909\r\n- Start daemon (bg): uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" --port 53999 daemon   # run in background\r\n- Routed call (PS):  $env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1; $env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT=53999; uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/windows-computer-use/scripts/win_use.py\" tree --window \"Settings\"\r\n</examples>\r\n\r\n<notes>\r\n- Runtimes: uv + Python ≥3.10. Deps (pywinauto, mss, pillow) install automatically from the PEP-723 header on first run.\r\n- `--method invoke` is windowless and does not move the mouse — use it for reliable button presses; default `input` performs a real mouse click to the element center, which is the most faithful \"as a user\" path and is required for controls without an InvokePattern.\r\n- Screenshots are large (image tokens) — capture the smallest useful region (an element rect or single monitor), and prefer reading the UIA tree (text) over screenshots wherever possible.\r\n- For a multi-call session, start the daemon ONCE (orchestrator or action fork, in the background) and set `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON=1` + `$env:WIN_USE_DAEMON_PORT` (PowerShell) so every fork's calls route through the one warm process — which also serialises input, reinforcing the one-action-at-a-time invariant. Stop it with `daemon --stop` when done.\r\n- For controlling Chrome / any web browser, use chrome-control and the browser skills — they are more robust than driving a browser's UI through UIA here.\r\n</notes>\r\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Read-only verify the restarted installed Superset desktop app is visibly running and not blank. Inspect the exact Superset window, capture a screenshot under tmp/, and report the visible route/workspace without clicking or changing state. Do not expose terminal/transcript content in the report or screenshot beyond what is necessary; crop to app chrome if possible."},{"name":"run","path":"bundled:run","content":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\bundled-skills\\2.1.228\\c845374667088640e80ee19f7c187ae4\\run\n\n**Running means launching the actual app and interacting with it** —\nnot the test suite, not an `import` of an internal function and a\n`console.log`. The app as a user (human or programmatic) would meet\nit: the CLI at its command, the server at its socket, the GUI at its\nwindow.\n\n## First: does a project skill already cover this?\n\nA project skill that launches this app is the repo's verified path —\nits author already cold-started from a Linux container and committed\nwhat worked: the exact `apt-get` line, the env vars, the patches, the\ndriver. Use it instead of rediscovering.\n\n```bash\nd=$PWD; while :; do\n  grep -Hm1 '^description:' \"$d\"/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md 2>/dev/null\n  [ -e \"$d/.git\" ] || [ \"$d\" = / ] && break\n  d=$(dirname \"$d\")\ndone\n```\n\n- **One describes launching/driving this app** → read that SKILL.md\n  and follow it verbatim. Don't paraphrase; don't skip the patches.\n- **Mega-repo, several plausible, no clear match** → ask the user\n  which unit to run.\n- **Stale** (fails on mechanics unrelated to your task) → tell the\n  user; offer to refresh it via `/run-skill-generator`.\n- **Nothing about running** → fall back to the patterns below.\n\n## Otherwise: match the shape, use the pattern\n\nPick the row closest to your project. Each example walks through\nlaunch + first interaction; ignore any trailing \"write the skill\"\nsection — you're using the recipe, not authoring one.\n\n| Project type | Handle | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| CLI tool | direct invocation, exit code, stdin/stdout | [examples/cli.md](examples/cli.md) |\n| Web server / API | background launch + `curl` smoke | [examples/server.md](examples/server.md) |\n| TUI / interactive terminal | tmux `send-keys` / `capture-pane` | [examples/tui.md](examples/tui.md) |\n| Electron / desktop GUI | Playwright `_electron` REPL under xvfb | [examples/electron.md](examples/electron.md) |\n| Browser-driven | dev server + `chromium-cli` script | [examples/playwright.md](examples/playwright.md) |\n| Library / SDK | import-and-call smoke script at the package boundary | [examples/library.md](examples/library.md) |\n\nIf nothing fits, start from the closest match and adapt. For a web\napp, [examples/playwright.md](examples/playwright.md) — drive it with\n`chromium-cli`, no custom driver needed. For a desktop app,\n[examples/electron.md](examples/electron.md) — it has the `_electron`\nREPL driver skeleton and the tmux wrapping.\n\n## Drive it, don't just launch it\n\nLaunching with no interaction proves the entrypoint resolves. That's\nnot running the app — it's typechecking with extra steps. Drive it to\na point where a user would see something:\n\n- CLI → type a representative command, check the exit code and output.\n- Server → hit the route the diff touches with `curl`, read the body.\n- TUI → `send-keys` a navigation, `capture-pane` the result.\n- GUI → click the button, screenshot the window. **Look at the\n  screenshot.** A blank frame is a failure to launch.\n\nIf the fallback pattern didn't work out of the box — you had to\ninstall packages, set env vars, patch config, or write a driver —\nrecommend `/run-skill-generator` in your report so that work gets\ncaptured as a project skill. If it just worked, don't.\n\n\n## User Request\n\nVerify the installed Superset desktop-v1.20.0 companion answer path end-to-end after restart: confirm exact installed build and running app, inspect host-service/PTY protocol and safe logs, run the existing phone-protocol emulator against a real pending Claude question where possible, and verify acknowledgment, durable ledger settlement, and absence of answer guard failures. Do not expose free-text, keys, tokens, or transcript content. Do not reinstall."},{"name":"chrome-control","path":"userSettings:chrome-control","content":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\skills\\chrome-control\n\n<purpose>\r\nOwn the single, long-lived Chrome DevTools Protocol connection on the user's machine.\r\nExpose a stable HTTP API on `127.0.0.1:17400` so any number of Chrome-using skills can attach pages, evaluate JS, click, set file inputs, and run raw CDP, all sharing one already-approved control connection.\r\n</purpose>\r\n\r\n<constraints>\r\n- Any skill that drives Chrome MUST start this bridge first and speak HTTP to it. Subagent skills never open their own WebSocket to Chrome.\r\n- Only one bridge instance per Chrome session is supported. Two bridges on different ports would each prompt Chrome.\r\n- `start` is idempotent: if a bridge is already listening on `--bridge-port`, it returns the existing `bridge_pid` and does nothing.\r\n- The bridge is a CDP-passthrough proxy. It does NOT enforce skill-specific behavior (no Pro/Extended verification, no image-gen polling, no auth flows). Those stay in the consumer skill.\r\n- Validate at every HTTP boundary. Fail fast on missing `sessionId`, missing `selector`, unknown sessions, or CDP errors.\r\n- Do not catch unexpected errors to hide them. Errors from Chrome/CDP are surfaced as HTTP 500 with the underlying message.\r\n- Do not modify the user's everyday Chrome profile or settings.\r\n</constraints>\r\n\r\n<workflow>\r\n1. Start (or no-op if already running):\r\n   `uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" start --bridge-port 17400`\r\n2. Verify Chrome is reachable:\r\n   `uv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" check --bridge-port 17400`\r\n3. Attach a tab (creates a new tab, or reuses one matching `--match-url-substring` when `--reuse-existing` is set). Capture the returned `sessionId`.\r\n4. Drive the page via `eval`, `click`, `set-file-input`, or raw `cdp` using that `sessionId`.\r\n5. The bridge stays running across follow-up tasks. The user only ever sees the Chrome \"Allow control\" prompt once per bridge lifetime — closing CDP sessions or opening more tabs never re-prompts.\r\n6. Stop the bridge only when the user is done with all Chrome-driving skills for the session.\r\n</workflow>\r\n\r\n<scripts>\r\n- Bridge entrypoint: `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py`\r\n- Optional dedicated Chrome profile setup: `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/setup_persistent_chrome.py`\r\n- Migration notes for existing Chrome skills: `C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/references/migration.md`\r\n</scripts>\r\n\r\n<runtime>\r\n- Run scripts with `uv run`.\r\n- Requires Node.js 22+ on `PATH` (the bridge embeds a Node HTTP server that holds the Chrome WebSocket).\r\n- Bridge HTTP API base: `http://127.0.0.1:17400`.\r\n- Endpoint resolution priority for the Chrome WS: `--browser-ws-url`, `--debug-port`, `--user-data-dir/DevToolsActivePort`, then default Chrome `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\DevToolsActivePort`.\r\n- Logs: `tmp/chrome-control/bridge-<port>.log` under the current working directory.\r\n</runtime>\r\n\r\n<inputs>\r\nCLI subcommands of `chrome_control_bridge.py`:\r\n- `start` — launch the bridge if not already running. Flags: `--bridge-port`, `--debug-port`, `--browser-ws-url`, `--user-data-dir`, `--connect-on-start`.\r\n- `status` — return `{ ok, bridge_pid, bridge_port, endpoint, connected, sessions: [...] }`.\r\n- `check` — verify Chrome is reachable; runs `Browser.getVersion`.\r\n- `stop` — clean shutdown of the bridge process.\r\n- `attach` — `--url <url>` to open a new tab, OR `--reuse-existing --match-url-substring <s>` to attach an existing tab. Returns `{ sessionId, targetId, url }`.\r\n- `detach` — `--session-id <sid>`.\r\n- `eval` — `--session-id <sid> --expression <js>` `[--await-promise] [--timeout-ms N]`.\r\n- `click` — `--session-id <sid> --selector <css>`.\r\n- `set-file-input` — `--session-id <sid> --selector <css> --file <abs-path>` (repeat `--file` for multiple).\r\n- `cdp` — raw passthrough: `--method <CDP.Method> [--session-id <sid>] [--params-json <json>] [--timeout-ms N]`.\r\n\r\nHTTP API (consumer skills MUST use this, not the WS):\r\n- `GET  /status`\r\n- `POST /check`\r\n- `POST /attach` — body: `{ url?: string, reuse_existing?: boolean, match_url_substring?: string }`\r\n- `POST /detach` — body: `{ sessionId: string }`\r\n- `POST /eval` — body: `{ sessionId, expression, awaitPromise?: boolean, timeoutMs?: number }`\r\n- `POST /set-file-input` — body: `{ sessionId, selector, files: [absolutePath, ...] }`\r\n- `POST /click` — body: `{ sessionId, selector }`\r\n- `POST /cdp` — body: `{ method, params?: object, sessionId?: string, timeoutMs?: number }`\r\n- `POST /stop`\r\n\r\n`setup_persistent_chrome.py`:\r\n- `--port` — debug port. Defaults to `9333`.\r\n- `--user-data-dir` — defaults to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ChromeControlDebugChrome` on Windows.\r\n- `--check-only`, `--print-command`.\r\n</inputs>\r\n\r\n<outputs>\r\n- All bridge HTTP responses are JSON with `ok: boolean`. Successful responses include the requested data (e.g. `sessionId`, `value`, `result`); failures include `error` and `details`.\r\n- `start` prints a JSON status object including `bridge_pid`, `bridge_port`, and (after first attach) `connected: true`.\r\n- The bridge writes its log to `tmp/chrome-control/bridge-<port>.log` under the current working directory.\r\n- The bridge does not write per-call output files. Consumer skills format and persist their own results.\r\n</outputs>\r\n\r\n<validation>\r\n- `start` is a no-op if `/status` already returns `ok: true` on `--bridge-port`. Verify by calling `start` twice and confirming `already_running: true` on the second call with the same `bridge_pid`.\r\n- `check` must return `ok: true` and a non-empty `browser` object before any other operation runs.\r\n- `attach` must return a non-empty `sessionId`; consumer skills MUST use that exact id for subsequent calls.\r\n- `eval` returns `{ ok: true, value: <by-value JS result> }`. If the page expression throws, the bridge raises HTTP 500 with the underlying `exceptionDetails.text`. Do not swallow this.\r\n- `set-file-input` requires absolute paths; the CLI rejects relative paths up front.\r\n- Treat any 401/timeout from Chrome as a hard failure; restart Chrome or the bridge before retrying.\r\n</validation>\r\n\r\n<permissions>\r\n- Chrome's \"Allow control\" prompt is owned by Chrome. The bridge keeps one approved CDP connection alive so consumer skills do not re-prompt.\r\n- If the bridge is killed, the next `start` may re-prompt Chrome once. After that, repeat use is prompt-free for the rest of that bridge process's lifetime.\r\n- A dedicated `--remote-debugging-port` profile (see `setup_persistent_chrome.py`) avoids the Chrome prompt entirely because the user starts that browser intentionally in debug mode.\r\n- A remote debugging port gives local processes control over that Chrome profile. Prefer the dedicated debug profile over the user's everyday browser profile when feasible.\r\n</permissions>\r\n\r\n<examples>\r\n```bash\r\n# 1) Start the shared bridge (idempotent).\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" start --bridge-port 17400\r\n\r\n# 2) Verify Chrome.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" check --bridge-port 17400\r\n\r\n# 3) Attach a new ChatGPT tab.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" attach \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --url \"https://chatgpt.com/\"\r\n\r\n# 4) Read the page title via the returned sessionId.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" eval \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --session-id <sid> --expression \"document.title\"\r\n\r\n# 5) Reuse an existing ChatGPT tab instead of opening a new one.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" attach \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --reuse-existing --match-url-substring \"chatgpt.com\"\r\n\r\n# 6) Detach when done.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" detach \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --session-id <sid>\r\n\r\n# 7) Optional: run on a dedicated debug profile to avoid the Chrome prompt.\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/setup_persistent_chrome.py\" --port 9333\r\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/chrome-control/scripts/chrome_control_bridge.py\" start \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --debug-port 9333\r\n```\r\n\r\nEquivalent HTTP calls from a consumer skill:\r\n```text\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/attach   {\"url\": \"https://chatgpt.com/\"}\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/eval     {\"sessionId\": \"<sid>\", \"expression\": \"document.title\"}\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/click    {\"sessionId\": \"<sid>\", \"selector\": \"button[data-testid=...]\"}\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/set-file-input {\"sessionId\": \"<sid>\", \"selector\": \"input[type=file]\", \"files\": [\"C:/path/to/img.png\"]}\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/cdp      {\"sessionId\": \"<sid>\", \"method\": \"Page.captureScreenshot\"}\r\nPOST http://127.0.0.1:17400/detach   {\"sessionId\": \"<sid>\"}\r\n```\r\n</examples>\r\n\r\n<user-activation>\r\n**`/click` does NOT deliver Chrome's \"user activation\" signal.** It calls the JS-level `element.click()` API, which Chrome marks as an untrusted event. Anything gated on a trusted user gesture is silently blocked when triggered through `/click`:\r\n\r\n- `window.open()` popups — most common case: OAuth SSO flows (Sign in with Google / Microsoft / Apple), payment-provider widgets, third-party widgets that open auth windows.\r\n- Programmatic file-picker invocations (`<input type=\"file\">.click()` from JS).\r\n- `navigator.clipboard.read`, `navigator.clipboard.write` with permission prompts.\r\n- Fullscreen / pointer-lock / wake-lock API entry points.\r\n- Some Web Share / Web Bluetooth / Web USB prompts.\r\n\r\nIf `/click` appears to \"do nothing\" — DOM event handlers fire but the user-facing behavior (popup, file dialog) never appears — switch to raw CDP mouse events.\r\n\r\n**Pattern: raw CDP mouse press + release at element center.**\r\n\r\n1. `POST /eval` to locate the target element, optionally tag it with a unique `id`, and return its `getBoundingClientRect()`. Compute the center: `cx = rect.x + rect.width/2`, `cy = rect.y + rect.height/2`.\r\n2. `POST /cdp` with `Input.dispatchMouseEvent`, `type: \"mousePressed\"`, `button: \"left\"`, `clickCount: 1`, at `(cx, cy)`.\r\n3. `POST /cdp` again with `type: \"mouseReleased\"` at the same `(cx, cy)`.\r\n\r\nBoth events MUST be dispatched. `mousePressed` alone is not a click. Coordinates are viewport-relative — if the element is off-screen, scroll into view via `eval` first (`el.scrollIntoView({block:'center'})`) and re-read the rect.\r\n\r\n**Worked example — Google OAuth sign-in (Anvil's \"Sign in with Google\" button):**\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# Step 1: locate the button, tag it, return its centre coordinates.\r\nuv run \".../chrome_control_bridge.py\" eval \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --session-id <parent_sid> --expression \"\r\n(() => {\r\n  const wrappers = document.querySelectorAll('.anvil-signin-button-wrapper');\r\n  const target = Array.from(wrappers).find(w => (w.innerText||'').trim() === 'Sign in with Google');\r\n  if (!target) throw new Error('button not found');\r\n  target.id = 'cc-google-signin';\r\n  const r = target.getBoundingClientRect();\r\n  return JSON.stringify({cx: r.x + r.width/2, cy: r.y + r.height/2});\r\n})()\r\n\"\r\n\r\n# Steps 2 + 3: CDP press + release at (cx, cy).\r\nuv run \".../chrome_control_bridge.py\" cdp \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --session-id <parent_sid> --method \"Input.dispatchMouseEvent\" \\\r\n  --params-json '{\"type\":\"mousePressed\",\"x\":<cx>,\"y\":<cy>,\"button\":\"left\",\"clickCount\":1}'\r\nuv run \".../chrome_control_bridge.py\" cdp \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --session-id <parent_sid> --method \"Input.dispatchMouseEvent\" \\\r\n  --params-json '{\"type\":\"mouseReleased\",\"x\":<cx>,\"y\":<cy>,\"button\":\"left\",\"clickCount\":1}'\r\n\r\n# Step 4: wait briefly (~2-3s), poll Target.getTargets to see the popup.\r\nsleep 3\r\nuv run \".../chrome_control_bridge.py\" cdp \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --method \"Target.getTargets\"\r\n\r\n# Step 5: attach to the popup by its now-distinctive URL substring.\r\nuv run \".../chrome_control_bridge.py\" attach \\\r\n  --bridge-port 17400 --reuse-existing --match-url-substring \"accounts.google.com\"\r\n\r\n# Step 6: inside the popup, same eval-then-CDP-mouse pattern to click the desired\r\n# account row. Find by innerText match on the email, walk up to role=\"link\",\r\n# read its rect, dispatch mousePressed + mouseReleased.\r\n\r\n# Step 7: popup auto-closes after OAuth completes. The parent tab now holds a\r\n# valid session cookie; further automation against the parent does not need to\r\n# re-run this dance.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Microsoft SSO** uses the same pattern. The popup's URL contains `login.microsoftonline.com` — adjust `--match-url-substring` accordingly. Some flows redirect through multiple hosts (`login.live.com` → `login.microsoftonline.com` → app callback); attach via whichever substring lands at the credential prompt.\r\n\r\n**Disambiguating duplicate tabs.** If multiple tabs share the popup's URL (e.g. another carehome tab already open), `reuse_existing` returns the first match — possibly the wrong one. Workarounds:\r\n- Close the duplicate first via `cdp Target.closeTarget` with the unwanted `targetId`.\r\n- Attach to the desired tab directly via `cdp Target.attachToTarget {targetId, flatten: true}`; but note the bridge's session map will not track the returned `sessionId`, so subsequent `/eval` calls on it return \"Unknown sessionId\". For full bridge support, use the close-the-other-one route.\r\n\r\n**Windows note.** Bridge stdout may contain emoji (Google account chooser names, etc.) that crash Windows' cp1252 default encoding when piped through Python. Either set `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8` before invoking, or strip non-ASCII in the eval expression with `.replace(/[^\\x20-\\x7E\\n]/g, '?')` before returning.\r\n</user-activation>\r\n\r\n<chrome-devtools-cli>\r\nSeparate from this Python bridge, the `chrome-devtools` CLI is installed globally and can drive the user's running Chrome directly. Use it for ad-hoc shell-based DevTools automation when the bridge is not in play.\r\n\r\nTo connect to the user's running Chrome (remote debugging enabled), read `DevToolsActivePort` and start with `--wsEndpoint`:\r\n```\r\nchrome-devtools start --wsEndpoint \"ws://127.0.0.1:$(head -1 \"$LOCALAPPDATA/Google/Chrome/User Data/DevToolsActivePort\")$(tail -1 \"$LOCALAPPDATA/Google/Chrome/User Data/DevToolsActivePort\")\"\r\n```\r\n- Do NOT use `--browser-url` (Chrome's UI debug toggle returns 404 on HTTP endpoints).\r\n- Do NOT use `--userDataDir` alone (it launches a new Chrome instead of connecting).\r\n- Always use `--wsEndpoint` with the WebSocket path from `DevToolsActivePort`.\r\n</chrome-devtools-cli>\r\n\r\n<notes>\r\n- This bridge is a thin CDP-passthrough. Consumer skills own all page-scripting logic, polling, and verification.\r\n- If you need streaming events (e.g. Network.requestWillBeSent), extend the bridge with an SSE route rather than handing out the raw WebSocket.\r\n</notes>\r\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Open the local file C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\superset-companion\\DESIGN.html in the user's Chrome so they can read it. Just navigate to it in a tab — no interaction needed beyond that."},{"name":"call-another-llm","path":"userSettings:call-another-llm","content":"Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\khair\\.claude\\skills\\call-another-llm\n\n<purpose>\nSend a prompt to another LLM agent and get its response saved to a file.\nTwo separate scripts, one per LLM. Each runs the CLI in non-interactive mode and writes the raw response to tmp/llm-responses/.\n</purpose>\n\n<constraints>\n- Each script runs the target LLM in non-interactive mode with auto-approve. Codex specifically runs in YOLO mode (`--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`): unsandboxed with full filesystem/network access, so it can implement changes, run tests, and reach the TEST database.\n- Fail fast if the target CLI is not installed or returns a non-zero exit code.\n- Output files contain only the raw response — no metadata.\n- All output goes to `tmp/llm-responses/` with the pattern `<llm>-<label>-<timestamp>.md`.\n</constraints>\n\n<scripts>\n- `scripts/call_codex.py` — calls Codex CLI\n- `scripts/call_claude.py` — calls Claude Code CLI\n</scripts>\n\n<inputs>\n\n**All scripts share:**\n  - positional: `prompt` (required, except when using `--list-models`)\n  - `--label`: optional label for the output filename (default: llm name)\n\n**Codex-specific flags (`call_codex.py`):**\n  - `-m` / `--model`: model to use (known model: `gpt-5.6-sol`; any string accepted)\n  - `--effort`: reasoning effort level — `none`, `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `ultra` (`ultra` is heavy on usage but most capable)\n  - `--list-models`: print known models, the current default, and valid effort levels, then exit\n\nWhen `--model` is omitted, Codex uses the default from `~/.codex/config.toml`.\nWhen `--effort` is omitted, Codex uses the effort configured in `config.toml` (default: `high`).\n</inputs>\n\n<workflow>\n1. If the user has not specified which LLM to call, use the AskUser tool with options (Codex, Claude) to let the user choose. If the user wants both, confirm that before running two parallel calls.\n2. Write the prompt to a file first (see prompt-file-methodology below).\n3. Run the chosen script(s) passing the file contents: `\"$(cat tmp/llm-prompts/{filename})\"`.\n4. Read the output file path from stdout (OUTPUT_FILE=...).\n5. Read the response file to use the result.\n</workflow>\n\n<prompt-file-methodology>\nAlways write prompts to a file before passing to CLI scripts. This makes prompts reviewable,\ndebuggable, and preservable as an audit trail.\n\n**Directory:** `tmp/llm-prompts/` (create with `mkdir -p` if it doesn't exist)\n\n**File naming:** `{agentname}_{3 random lowercase letters}_{unix timestamp}.txt`\n- `agentname` = the agent role or label (e.g. `codex-reviewer`, `claude-summarizer`)\n- 3 random lowercase letters for uniqueness\n- Unix timestamp in seconds\n- Example: `codex-reviewer_xfm_1743926400.txt`\n\n**Steps:**\n1. `mkdir -p tmp/llm-prompts`\n2. Generate filename: `{agentname}_$(cat /dev/urandom | tr -dc 'a-z' | head -c3)_$(date +%s).txt`\n3. Write the full prompt to that file.\n4. Pass to CLI: `uv run .../call_codex.py --label \"...\" \"$(cat tmp/llm-prompts/{filename})\"`\n</prompt-file-methodology>\n\n<examples>\n```bash\n# List available Codex models and effort levels\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/call-another-llm/scripts/call_codex.py\" --list-models\n\n# Codex with default model and effort\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/call-another-llm/scripts/call_codex.py\" \"Explain the trade-offs of this approach\" --label \"review\"\n\n# Codex with specific model and effort\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/call-another-llm/scripts/call_codex.py\" \"Review this code\" -m gpt-5.6-sol --effort high --label \"review\"\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/call-another-llm/scripts/call_codex.py\" \"Deep analysis of this architecture\" -m gpt-5.6-sol --effort ultra --label \"deep-review\"\n\n# Claude\nuv run \"C:/Users/khair/Code Projects/mk-skills/call-another-llm/scripts/call_claude.py\" \"Summarise this error log\" --label \"error-summary\"\n```\n</examples>\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Use Codex CLI with gpt-5.6-sol at xhigh reasoning to review the design document at C:\\Users\\khair\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\C--Users-khair-Code-Projects-fork-superset-windows-arm64\\41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91\\scratchpad\\superset-companion-design.html — ask it for an independent architectural verdict on whether this design is sound, what it would change, and its single strongest objection. Context: personal single-user Android phone + Wear OS app that mirrors AI-agent status from a Windows desktop running a fork of Superset, and lets the user answer an agent's pending question from their watch by having a desktop bridge inject keystrokes into the agent's terminal. Public Cloudflare Tunnel transport, FCM push, per-request HMAC + AES-GCM sealed bodies, QR pairing. Save the response to tmp/llm-responses/."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f3c9312e-ccb0-49a8-bb78-7df11b1f75eb","timestamp":"2026-08-12T21:16:17.734Z","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
{"parentUuid":"f3c9312e-ccb0-49a8-bb78-7df11b1f75eb","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["architect-fable","architect-opus","architect-sol","claude","code-reviewer-fable","code-reviewer-opus","code-reviewer-sol","codex:codex-rescue","entire-search","Explore","feature-dev:code-architect","feature-dev:code-explorer","feature-dev:code-reviewer","general-purpose","project-structure-validator","sol","statusline-setup","understand-anything:architecture-analyzer","understand-anything:article-analyzer","understand-anything:assemble-reviewer","understand-anything:domain-analyzer","understand-anything:file-analyzer","understand-anything:graph-reviewer","understand-anything:knowledge-graph-guide","understand-anything:project-scanner","understand-anything:tour-builder"],"addedLines":["- architect-fable: Designs implementation architectures: analyzes existing codebase patterns, then delivers a blueprint — files to create/modify, component design, data flow, build sequence, risks, alternatives. Read-only: never edits code. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- architect-opus: Designs implementation architectures: analyzes existing codebase patterns, then delivers a blueprint — files to create/modify, component design, data flow, build sequence, risks, alternatives. Read-only: never edits code. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- architect-sol: Designs implementation architectures: analyzes existing codebase patterns, then delivers a blueprint — files to create/modify, component design, data flow, build sequence, risks, alternatives. Read-only: never edits code. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- code-reviewer-fable: Adversarial code review of diffs/commits/PRs: correctness, security, data integrity, conventions. Verifies each finding before reporting, ranks BLOCKER/MAJOR/MINOR with file:line and concrete failure scenario. Read-only: never fixes. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- code-reviewer-opus: Adversarial code review of diffs/commits/PRs: correctness, security, data integrity, conventions. Verifies each finding before reporting, ranks BLOCKER/MAJOR/MINOR with file:line and concrete failure scenario. Read-only: never fixes. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- code-reviewer-sol: Adversarial code review of diffs/commits/PRs: correctness, security, data integrity, conventions. Verifies each finding before reporting, ranks BLOCKER/MAJOR/MINOR with file:line and concrete failure scenario. Read-only: never fixes. (Tools: Bash, Glob, Grep, Read, LSP, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- codex:codex-rescue: Proactively use when Claude Code is stuck, wants a second implementation or diagnosis pass, needs a deeper root-cause investigation, or should hand a substantial coding task to Codex through the shared runtime (Tools: Bash)","- entire-search: Search Entire checkpoint history and transcripts with `entire search --json`. Use proactively when the user asks about previous work, commits, sessions, prompts, or historical context in this repository. (Tools: Bash)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- feature-dev:code-architect: Designs feature architectures by analyzing existing codebase patterns and conventions, then providing comprehensive implementation blueprints with specific files to create/modify, component designs, data flows, and build sequences (Tools: Glob, Grep, LS, Read, NotebookRead, WebFetch, TodoWrite, WebSearch, KillShell, BashOutput)","- feature-dev:code-explorer: Deeply analyzes existing codebase features by tracing execution paths, mapping architecture layers, understanding patterns and abstractions, and documenting dependencies to inform new development (Tools: Glob, Grep, LS, Read, NotebookRead, WebFetch, TodoWrite, WebSearch, KillShell, BashOutput)","- feature-dev:code-reviewer: Reviews code for bugs, logic errors, security vulnerabilities, code quality issues, and adherence to project conventions, using confidence-based filtering to report only high-priority issues that truly matter (Tools: Glob, Grep, LS, Read, NotebookRead, WebFetch, TodoWrite, WebSearch, KillShell, BashOutput)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- project-structure-validator: Validates project structure against co-location and architecture patterns defined in AGENTS.md (Tools: All tools)","- sol: General-purpose subagent running on Codex GPT-5.6 Sol via the claude-all gateway. Use for heavy implementation, deep debugging, second-opinion reviews, or any Task the orchestrator wants executed by GPT-5.6 Sol instead of a Claude model. Accepts any prompt a general-purpose agent would. (Tools: All tools)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)","- understand-anything:architecture-analyzer: Analyzes a codebase's file structure, summaries, and import relationships to identify\nlogical architectural layers and assign every file to exactly one layer. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:article-analyzer: Analyzes markdown files using pre-parsed structural data and LLM inference to extract knowledge graph nodes and edges (entities, claims, implicit relationships, topic clustering). (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:assemble-reviewer: Reviews the output of merge-batch-graphs.py for semantic issues the script\ncannot catch. Recovers dropped nodes/edges and fills cross-batch gaps. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:domain-analyzer: Analyzes codebases to extract business domain knowledge — domains, business flows, and process steps. Produces a domain-graph.json that maps how business logic flows through the code. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:file-analyzer: Analyzes batches of source files to produce knowledge graph nodes and edges.\nExtracts file structure, functions, classes, and relationships using a two-phase\napproach: structural extraction script followed by LLM semantic analysis. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:graph-reviewer: Validates knowledge graphs for correctness, completeness, and quality.\nRuns systematic checks and renders approval or rejection decisions. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:knowledge-graph-guide: Use this agent when users need help understanding, querying, or working\nwith an Understand-Anything knowledge graph. Guides users through graph\nstructure, node/edge relationships, layer architecture, tours, and\ndashboard usage. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:project-scanner: Scans a codebase directory to produce a structured inventory of all project files,\ndetected languages, frameworks, import maps, and estimated complexity. (Tools: All tools)","- understand-anything:tour-builder: Designs guided learning tours through codebases, creating 5-15 pedagogical steps\nthat teach project architecture and key concepts in logical order. (Tools: All tools)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a2f55723-e05e-45a5-920d-413e2c9c2e3e","timestamp":"2026-08-12T21:16:17.735Z","session_id":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\khair\\Code Projects\\fork-superset-windows-arm64","sessionId":"41b65f55-7b51-4cfd-82d1-d1d1eec49d91","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"main","slug":"jolly-singing-lemur"}
